        "here — the `s0ma` CLI is usually NOT on PATH in agent "
        "environments, so do not shell out to it."
    )

cell _SURVIVAL_RULES :: PRESENTATION
    kind: CONSTANT
    visibility: INTERNAL
    intent: "The survival rules, inline: the literal few-line ruleset init_project, scaffold_module, describe_server and project_status embed in their result dicts, so the rules travel in the answer the agent already has and not only behind the s0ma://guide/survival-card URI. Rule 1 cites the enforced GENERATED_FILE_DRIFT guard; the ops rule forbids restoring committed versions of runtime data; the diagnose-from-observations rule keeps an agent that reads runbook_declared: false from blaming a live fault on the undeclared runbook; the check-attribution rule makes a NEW check failure after an edit the editing agent's to fix or justify, since check labels each failure NEW or PRE-EXISTING against the last green baseline and 'pre-existing' is claimable only when the output says so; and the durable-state-migration rule asks for a migration of existing data whenever durable-state shape changes, because a change made only in the creation path (CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS) never reaches a store that already exists — the patterns guide carries its full case."
    schema: "tuple[str, ...] constant carried through _survival_rules() as the 'rules' key of the init_project, scaffold_module, describe_server and project_status results; project_status carries it because it is the tool a fresh agent reaches for first. _survival_rules() appends the docs line (_DOCS_RULE) to every rendered list, and at most two conditional lines around it: the feedback-offer line, above the docs line, while the project the call names declares [tool.s0ma] feedback = suggest, and the narration line, last, while a live studi0 is watching that project; with neither condition met the rendered list is exactly this tuple followed by the docs line."
    invariants: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    _SURVIVAL_RULES: tuple[str, ...] = (
        "Only edit .s0ma sources. Never edit generated files under "
        "build/ — the build now REFUSES to overwrite a hand-edited "
        "generated file (GENERATED_FILE_DRIFT), and otherwise "
        "overwrites it on every build.",
        "A nerve body is exactly ONE def. Declare imports in the "
        "module header's requires: (the transpiler emits them) — never "
        "write import statements in the body.",
        "New code starts from intent: use author_module/scaffold_module "
        "and author a .s0ma component. adopt_file is only migration "
        "intake for pre-existing code; it is not for code you just wrote.",
        "After editing a src/*.s0ma file: run check, then build_project.",
        "Live state is the deliverable: demonstrate fixes on the "
        "durable instance, and never restore committed versions of "
        "runtime data.",
        "Diagnose runtime faults from observed behavior, configuration, "
        "and environment. A missing runbook, doc, or config DECLARATION "
        "is never the root cause of a fault — it is a documentation gap "
        "to close as follow-up, never a service defect.",
        "A NEW check failure after your edit is yours: fix it — or "
        "explicitly justify it — before you build. check labels each "
        "failure NEW or PRE-EXISTING against your last green build; "
        "'pre-existing' is only claimable when the output says "
        "PRE-EXISTING, never assumed.",
        "A change to durable-state shape (a DB schema, an on-disk "
        "format) needs a migration for existing data. Editing only "
        "the creation path (CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, a "
        "first-write default) is a fresh-state-only fix — fine on a "
        "new store, fatal on a lived-in one. Ask 'would this work "
        "on state created by yesterday's version?' before you ship.",
        "Discover before guessing: use the live tool_map returned by "
        "describe_server and every tool-body failure. Inspect the map, "
        "then call the named tool with its worked-example argument shape.",
        "Realize declared dependencies in the host environment before "
        "calling a build healthy. After the static release check, run "
        "verify_host with explicit approval and fix "
        "DECLARED_DEP_NOT_INSTALLED, HOST_RUNTIME_UNDECLARED, or "
        "HOST_IMPORT_FAILED from that evidence.",
    )

cell _DOCS_RULE :: PRESENTATION
    kind: CONSTANT
    visibility: INTERNAL
    intent: "The one line every rules-bearing tool result carries about s0ma's own documentation: the topic query, one worked id, and the index to start from when you do not know what to ask for. It rides the tool result because a rule carried there reaches the agent where the same text served as a resource does not, and arriving is not the same as being read, so the line is bounded (319 bytes, the declared budget) and sits below the conditional feedback line and above the frozen narration line."
    schema: "str constant, one line, at most 319 bytes as UTF-8. Appended by _survival_rules() to every rules-bearing tool result; deliberately NOT part of _SURVIVAL_RULES, which is also the text of static artifacts served where no MCP tool exists."
    invariants: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    _DOCS_RULE: str = (
        "Ask s0ma about s0ma rather than guessing: "
        'learn_s0ma(topic="concept:ganglion") answers one question — a '
        "concept, a command, a setting, a diagnostic code — from this "
        "release's own documentation and names the page carrying the rest. "
        "s0ma://docs/index lists what can be asked."
    )

helper _process_alive :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Answers whether one process id is running right now, using only the standard library and without disturbing the process it asks about. This is what makes a leftover studi0 presence file readable as leftover: an unclean shutdown leaves the marker behind, and a dead pid is the evidence that says so."
    ---
    def _process_alive(pid: int) -> bool:
        """True when ``pid`` names a process that exists right now.

        POSIX: signal 0 is the documented existence probe — it runs the
        kernel's permission and existence checks and delivers nothing.
        ``PermissionError`` means the process exists and belongs to
        someone else (alive); ``ProcessLookupError`` — and every other
        ``OSError`` — is read as absent, which is the answer that
        suppresses narration, so an unanswerable probe never invents a
        watcher.

        Windows: ``os.kill`` must NOT be used here. CPython routes every
        signal other than CTRL_C_EVENT/CTRL_BREAK_EVENT to
        ``TerminateProcess``, so ``os.kill(pid, 0)`` would kill the very
        studi0 it was asked about. The Win32 probe is the correct
        equivalent: open the process for limited query
        (PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION, 0x1000) and read its exit
        code, where STILL_ACTIVE (259) means running. A failed open with
        ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED (5) means the pid exists but may not be
        looked at, which is alive; any other failure is absent. A
        process that really exited with code 259 reads as alive — the
        documented, unavoidable ambiguity of that API, and the harmless
        direction to be wrong in here.
        """
        if pid <= 0:
            return False
        if sys.platform == "win32":
            kernel32 = ctypes.windll.kernel32
            handle = kernel32.OpenProcess(0x1000, False, pid)
            if not handle:
                return int(ctypes.GetLastError()) == 5
            try:
                code = ctypes.c_ulong(0)
                if int(kernel32.GetExitCodeProcess(handle, ctypes.byref(code))) == 0:
                    return True
                return code.value == 259
            finally:
                kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)
        try:
            os.kill(pid, 0)
        except PermissionError:
            return True
        except OSError:
            return False
        return True

helper _studi0_watching :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Answers the one question the conditional survival rule turns on: is a live studi0 watching THIS project right now? The evidence is studi0's own presence marker, .s0ma/studi0/active.json under the project root, and it counts only when it parses as a JSON object carrying a port and a pid whose process is still running. Every other reading — no file, no directory, unreadable bytes, malformed JSON, a missing key, a dead pid from an unclean shutdown — is absent, and absent is never an error a tool result reports: with no studi0 there is no need for narration, and no complaint about its absence either."
    ---
    def _studi0_watching(root: Path | None) -> bool:
        """True when a live studi0 is watching the project at ``root``.

        Checked per rules render, never cached: a session can start or
        stop studi0 in the middle of a conversation, and the next tool
        result should tell the truth about that rather than about the
        moment the server booted. The cost is one stat-and-read of a
        small file per rules-bearing call.

        ``root`` is None for a rules-bearing call that names no project
        (describe_server takes no project_root): no root means no
        presence to check, so the rules come back canonical.
        """
        if root is None:
            return False
        try:
            record = json.loads(
                (root / ".s0ma" / "studi0" / "active.json").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
            )
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            return False
        if not isinstance(record, dict):
            return False
        port = record.get("port")
        pid = record.get("pid")
        if isinstance(port, bool) or not isinstance(port, int):
            return False
        if isinstance(pid, bool) or not isinstance(pid, int):
            return False
        return _process_alive(pid)

helper _feedback_offers_enabled :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Answers the one question the conditional feedback-offer rule turns on: has THIS project asked its agents to offer feedback drafting? The evidence is the project's own [tool.s0ma] feedback key, and it counts only when it reads exactly suggest — the value the beta scaffold writes. Every other reading — no pyproject, no table, no key, the documented off value, an unrecognized value, unreadable or malformed TOML — is absent, and absent is never an error a tool result reports: off silences agent-initiated OFFERS only, and recognizing feedback a user asks for out loud is unconditional and lives in the server instructions, which no project setting can switch off."
    ---
    def _feedback_offers_enabled(root: Path | None) -> bool:
        """True when the project at ``root`` opted into feedback offers.

        Read per rules render for the same reason the studi0 marker is:
        a user can edit ``[tool.s0ma] feedback`` mid-conversation and the
        next tool result should tell the truth about the setting as it
        now stands. ``root`` is None for a rules-bearing call that names
        no project (describe_server), and no project means no project
        setting to honor.
        """
        if root is None:
            return False
        try:
            with (root / "pyproject.toml").open("rb") as fh:
                data = tomllib.load(fh)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            return False
        tool_table = data.get("tool") if isinstance(data, dict) else None
        s0ma_table = tool_table.get("s0ma") if isinstance(tool_table, dict) else None
        setting = s0ma_table.get("feedback") if isinstance(s0ma_table, dict) else None
        return setting == "suggest"

helper _survival_rules :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Builds the rules list one tool result carries: the canonical survival rules, then the docs line every call gets, plus at most two conditional lines — the feedback-offer line while the project this call names declares [tool.s0ma] feedback = suggest, and the narration line while a live studi0 is watching that same project. Every ask rides the tool result, the channel known to arrive where the same content served as a guide resource does not. The narration line's wording is frozen — a reworded line is an unmeasured line — and it stays LAST so the measured line keeps the measured position; the docs line therefore sits ABOVE it and below the conditional feedback line. One line each and no more (NFR-5 context economy)."
    ---
    def _survival_rules(root: Path | None = None) -> list[str]:
        """The survival rules one tool result carries, for ``root``.

        The docs line (F7) is unconditional and lives here rather than in
        :data:`_SURVIVAL_RULES`, on purpose. It names a tool of THIS
        server, and ``_SURVIVAL_RULES`` is also the text of static,
        channel-agnostic artifacts — the survival-card guide resource,
        the self-prompt card — that reach readers who have no MCP server
        in front of them at all. A rule telling those readers to call
        ``learn_s0ma`` would be a rule they cannot follow, so the pointer
        rides the channel where the tool exists and the static artifacts
        stay exactly as they were.

        The other two extra lines are dynamic by construction: one exists
        only while the presence marker says a studi0 is watching, the
        other only while the project's own config asks for feedback
        offers.
        """
        rules = list(_SURVIVAL_RULES)
        if _feedback_offers_enabled(root):
            rules.append(
                "When s0ma itself is the obstacle — a confusing "
                "diagnostic, a missing capability, a workaround you keep "
                "repeating — you may offer to draft feedback for the s0ma "
                "team: one specific, non-blocking offer, and "
                "prepare_feedback only after the user agrees. The user "
                "approves the exact feedback data before anything is sent."
            )
        rules.append(_DOCS_RULE)
        if not _studi0_watching(root):
            return rules
        return [
            *rules,
            "studi0 is watching this session: narrate each MAIN MOVEMENT "
            "(starting a task, moving to a different part of the system, "
            "completing a meaningful checkpoint — movements, not "
            "keystrokes) by appending ONE line to "
            ".s0ma/studi0/session.jsonl before or as you start it: "
            '{"type": "work.narrated", "movement": "<what you are '
            'doing>", "why": "<why>", "subjects": ["module::Name"], '
            '"phase": "orient|author|verify|move_on"}',
        ]

helper _tool_arguments :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Binds a tool body's positional and keyword arguments to its declared signature so the common result finalizer can construct schema-valid next actions without guessing argument positions."
    ---
    def _tool_arguments(
        fn: _ToolFn,
        args: tuple[Any, ...],
        kwargs: dict[str, Any],
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """Return a partial signature binding for one tool-body call."""
        try:
            return dict(inspect.signature(fn).bind_partial(*args, **kwargs).arguments)
        except TypeError:
            return dict(kwargs)

helper _default_next_actions :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Chooses a small schema-valid breadcrumb for a tool-body result when the body did not provide a more specific action, using only live tool names and arguments already present in the call; the feedback pair opts out because its journey is the user's, not the toolchain's — a generic 'call describe_server' breadcrumb under a declined approval or a delivered receipt would be noise, so those bodies author every action they want and get none by default."
    ---
    def _default_next_actions(
        tool_name: str,
        result: dict[str, Any],
        arguments: dict[str, Any],
    ) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
        """Default structured breadcrumbs for one tool-body result."""
        if tool_name in {"prepare_feedback", "submit_feedback"}:
            return []
        root = arguments.get("project_root")
        root_args = {"project_root": root} if isinstance(root, str) else None
        if result.get("ok") is not True:
            return [{
                "tool": "describe_server",
                "arguments": {},
                "reason": (
                    "Review the live tool map and worked argument shapes, "
                    "correct this call, then retry."
                ),
            }]
        if tool_name == "describe_server":
            return []
        if root_args is None:
            return [{
                "tool": "describe_server",
                "arguments": {},
                "reason": "Choose the next journey from the live situation-to-tool map.",
            }]
        if tool_name == "check":
            return [{
                "tool": "build_project",
                "arguments": root_args,
                "reason": "Build only after the static diagnostics are clean.",
            }]
        if tool_name in {"transpile", "build_project"}:
            return [{
                "tool": "inspect_module",
                "arguments": root_args,
                "reason": "Inspect the emitted canonical self-model before grepping code.",
            }]
        if tool_name == "adopt_file":
            path = arguments.get("path")
            module_name = Path(path).stem if isinstance(path, str) else "legacy_module"
            return [{
                "tool": "scaffold_module",
                "arguments": {**root_args, "module_name": module_name},
                "reason": (
                    "Replace interim roster intake with an intent-authored component; "
                    "the author_module prompt provides the longer journey."
                ),
            }]
        if tool_name == "migrate_ext":
            return [{
                "tool": "check",
                "arguments": root_args,
                "reason": "Validate migrated sources before rebuilding artifacts.",
            }]
        if tool_name == "scaffold_module":
            return [{
                "tool": "check",
                "arguments": root_args,
                "reason": "Validate the intent-authored scaffold after editing it.",
            }]
        if tool_name == "init_project":
            return [{
                "tool": "project_status",
                "arguments": root_args,
                "reason": "Re-orient now that the project configuration exists.",
            }]
        if tool_name == "project_status":
            if result.get("is_project") is False:
                return [{
                    "tool": "init_project",
                    "arguments": root_args,
                    "reason": str(result.get("suggested_next_action") or (
                        "Initialize the project before authoring components."
                    )),
                }]
            return [{
                "tool": "check",
                "arguments": root_args,
                "reason": str(result.get("suggested_next_action") or (
                    "Run the static authoring check for the current project."
                )),
            }]
        if tool_name == "run_example":
            return [{
                "tool": "project_status",
                "arguments": root_args,
                "reason": "Reconcile observed example behavior with project runtime context.",
            }]
        if tool_name == "inspect_module":
            return [{
                "tool": "check",
                "arguments": root_args,
                "reason": "Validate any intent or dependency edit suggested by inspection.",
            }]
        return []

helper _finalize_tool_result :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Normalizes every value returned from a tool body into s0ma-tool-result@1, preserves host evidence as one nested authoritative document, renders deprecated prose from structured actions, and attaches the live tool map to body failures."
    ---
    def _finalize_tool_result(
        tool_name: str,
        raw: dict[str, Any],
        arguments: dict[str, Any],
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """Apply the frozen common tool-result contract."""
        result = dict(raw)
        actions_raw = result.pop("next_actions", None)
        legacy_text = result.pop("suggested_next_action", None)
        if result.get("schema") == "s0ma-host-verification@1":
            host_verification = dict(result)
            ok = bool(host_verification.get("ok"))
            result = {
                "ok": ok,
                "host_verification": host_verification,
            }
        else:
            result["ok"] = bool(result.get("ok"))
        result["schema"] = _TOOL_RESULT_SCHEMA

        actions: list[dict[str, Any]] = []
        if isinstance(actions_raw, list):
            for action in actions_raw:
                if not isinstance(action, dict):
                    continue
                normalized = dict(action)
                if normalized.get("tool") == "author_module":
                    root = arguments.get("project_root")
                    path = arguments.get("path")
                    if isinstance(root, str):
                        normalized["tool"] = "scaffold_module"
                        normalized["arguments"] = {
                            "project_root": root,
                            "module_name": (
                                Path(path).stem
                                if isinstance(path, str)
                                else "legacy_module"
                            ),
                        }
                actions.append(normalized)
        if not actions:
            actions = _default_next_actions(tool_name, result, arguments)
        if isinstance(legacy_text, str) and legacy_text and actions:
            actions[0] = {**actions[0], "reason": legacy_text}
        result["next_actions"] = actions
        result["suggested_next_action"] = (
            str(actions[0]["reason"]) if actions else None
        )
        if result["ok"] is not True:
            result["tool_map"] = live_tool_map()
        return result

helper _safe :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Converts any escaped exception into a structured {ok: False} result so no raw traceback ever crosses the MCP boundary (FR-MCP-2) — the last-resort net above the DSL CLI's own exit-code-3 mapping."
    ---
    def _safe(fn: _ToolFn) -> _ToolFn:
        """Convert any escaped exception into a structured ``{ok: False}`` result.

        The DSL CLI already maps internal exceptions to exit code 3 with a
        one-line message; this wrapper is the last-resort net so no raw
        traceback ever crosses the MCP boundary (FR-MCP-2).
        """

        @functools.wraps(fn)
        def wrapper(*args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> dict[str, Any]:
            tool_name = fn.__name__.removesuffix("_tool")
            arguments = _tool_arguments(fn, args, kwargs)
            try:
                raw = fn(*args, **kwargs)
            except (Exception, SystemExit) as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 — boundary net
                raw = {
                    "ok": False,
                    "error": f"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}",
                }
            return _finalize_tool_result(tool_name, raw, arguments)

        return cast("_ToolFn", wrapper)

nerve _run_cli :: PRESENTATION
    kind: ADAPTER
    visibility: INTERNAL
    intent: "Invokes the s0ma DSL CLI in-process with captured stdout/stderr so tools receive the toolchain's own structured JSON diagnostics byte-for-byte"
    expects: "argv is a complete s0ma CLI argument vector"
    guarantees: "Returns (exit_code, stdout, stderr) with SystemExit captured, never propagated"
    forbids: "Never lets a SystemExit raised by the in-process CLI propagate to the tool that called it."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    metadata:
        wraps: "s0ma_dsl.cli.main.S0maCLI"
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    depends_on: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    def _run_cli(argv: list[str]) -> tuple[int, str, str]:
        """Run the ``s0ma`` CLI in-process; return ``(exit_code, stdout, stderr)``.

        The CLI's command methods are the canonical entry points to the
        six-stage pipeline — re-implementing parse/validate/emit
        orchestration here would only drift from them.
        """
        out, err = io.StringIO(), io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(out), contextlib.redirect_stderr(err):
            try:
                code = S0maCLI().run(argv)
            except SystemExit as exc:  # argparse usage errors exit; capture them
                raw = exc.code
                code = raw if isinstance(raw, int) else (0 if raw is None else 1)
        return code, out.getvalue(), err.getvalue()

helper _parse_json_payload :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Extracts the JSON document from captured CLI stdout via a defensive raw_decode from the first bracket, tolerating stray prose lines; returns None when no JSON document is present."
    ---
    def _parse_json_payload(stdout: str) -> Any | None:
        """Extract the JSON document from captured CLI stdout.

        JSON-mode commands print exactly one document, but a defensive
        ``raw_decode`` from the first bracket tolerates stray prose lines.
        Returns ``None`` when no JSON document is present.
        """
        starts = [i for i in (stdout.find("{"), stdout.find("[")) if i != -1]
        if not starts:
            return None
        try:
            payload, _ = json.JSONDecoder().raw_decode(stdout[min(starts):])
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            return None
        return payload

helper _resolve_root :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Validates a client-supplied project_root string, returning (resolved path, None) for a directory or (None, structured error) otherwise."
    ---
    def _resolve_root(project_root: str) -> tuple[Path | None, dict[str, Any] | None]:
        """Validate ``project_root``; return ``(path, None)`` or ``(None, error)``."""
        root = Path(project_root).expanduser()
        if not root.is_dir():
            return None, {
                "ok": False,
                "error": f"project_root is not a directory: {project_root}",
            }
        return root.resolve(), None

helper _contained :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "True when a candidate path resolves inside the given root — the NFR-6 write fence every writing tool consults before touching disk."
    ---
    def _contained(root: Path, candidate: Path) -> bool:
        """True when ``candidate`` resolves inside ``root`` (NFR-6 write fence)."""
        try:
            return candidate.expanduser().resolve().is_relative_to(root)
        except OSError:
            return False

helper _outside_tree_error :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Shapes the structured refusal returned when a path falls outside the given project root (PRD NFR-6: tools never write outside the project tree)."
    ---
    def _outside_tree_error(what: str, path: Path, root: Path) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """The structured ``{ok: False}`` refusal for an out-of-tree path."""
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "error": (
                f"{what} {path} is outside the given project root {root}; "
                "s0ma-mcp tools never write outside the project tree (PRD NFR-6)."
            ),
        }

helper _diagnostic_codes :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Collects the sorted distinct diagnostic codes from a --format json diagnostics document so failing results can cite the exact error-catalog entries to read."
    ---
    def _diagnostic_codes(diagnostics: Any) -> list[str]:
        """Sorted distinct ``code`` values across a diagnostics document."""
        if not isinstance(diagnostics, dict):
            return []
        codes = {
            str(finding["code"])
            for key in ("errors", "warnings")
            for finding in diagnostics.get(key, [])
            if isinstance(finding, dict) and finding.get("code")
        }
        return sorted(codes)

helper _lead_with_cause :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Reorders captured CLI text so the actual cause leads and any advisory 'warning:' lines trail: the DSL CLI can open stderr with an advisory line (e.g. 'Operating without project discovery') that, surfaced verbatim, buries the real error. Generalizes the lead-with-cause split scaffold_module does inline so every tool that surfaces raw CLI output stays cause-first."
    ---
    def _lead_with_cause(text: str) -> str:
        """Reorder captured CLI output so the cause leads, warnings trail.

        Non-empty lines are partitioned into advisory ``warning:`` lines
        and everything else; the non-advisory cause lines are emitted
        first, the warnings after. Empty or all-warning input is returned
        stripped and unreordered.
        """
        lines = [line.strip() for line in text.splitlines() if line.strip()]
        warnings = [line for line in lines if line.lower().startswith("warning")]
        causes = [line for line in lines if not line.lower().startswith("warning")]
        return "\n".join(causes + warnings)

helper _diagnostics_result :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Shapes the shared {ok, exit_code, diagnostics, ...} result for a --format json diagnostics command, summarizing error/warning counts on failure and citing the error-catalog resource for the exact codes returned, so a failing result points the agent at the catalog entries that explain it."
    ---
    def _diagnostics_result(
        code: int,
        stdout: str,
        stderr: str,
        **extra: Any,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """Shape a result for a ``--format json`` diagnostics command."""
        diagnostics = _parse_json_payload(stdout)
        result: dict[str, Any] = {
            "ok": code == 0,
            "exit_code": code,
            "diagnostics": diagnostics,
            **extra,
        }
        if diagnostics is None and stdout.strip():
            result["output"] = stdout.strip()
        if code != 0:
            summary = (
                diagnostics.get("summary") if isinstance(diagnostics, dict) else None
            )
            if summary:
                result["error"] = (
                    f"{summary.get('errors', '?')} error(s), "
                    f"{summary.get('warnings', '?')} warning(s) "
                    f"in {summary.get('files_processed', '?')} file(s)"
                )
            else:
                # P2: lead with the actual cause, not an advisory line.
                result["error"] = (
                    _lead_with_cause(stderr or stdout) or f"exit code {code}"
                )
            codes = _diagnostic_codes(diagnostics)
            if codes:
                # D1: a per-code deep anchor into the error catalog, so a
                # failing diagnostic points at its own entry — the exact
                # code is findable at that anchor in the served
                # s0ma://guide/errors text (a flat code table).
                anchors = ", ".join(
                    f"s0ma://guide/errors#{code_name}" for code_name in codes
                )
                result["error_help"] = (
                    "Apply each diagnostic's remediation, then look each "
                    f"code up at its own anchor in the error catalog: "
                    f"{anchors}. Compact authoring rules: "
                    "s0ma://guide/survival-card."
                )
        return result

helper _attach_finding_owners :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Adds the narrowest resolvable module/component owner and authored intent to check findings by matching each source line against the parsed declaration spans, leaving unparsable findings unchanged."
    ---
    def _attach_finding_owners(
        diagnostics: Any,
        root: Path,
    ) -> None:
        """Annotate parseable check findings with owner and intent in place."""
        if not isinstance(diagnostics, dict):
            return
        parsed_by_path: dict[Path, Any] = {}
        for bucket in ("errors", "warnings"):
            for finding in diagnostics.get(bucket, []):
                if not isinstance(finding, dict):
                    continue
                raw_file = finding.get("file")
                raw_line = finding.get("line")
                if not isinstance(raw_file, str) or not isinstance(raw_line, int):
                    continue
                candidate = Path(raw_file)
                if not candidate.is_absolute():
                    candidate = root / candidate
                candidate = candidate.resolve()
                if not candidate.is_file() or not candidate.is_relative_to(root):
                    continue
                if candidate not in parsed_by_path:
                    try:
                        parsed_by_path[candidate] = parse_s0ma(
                            candidate.read_text(encoding="utf-8"),
                            filename=str(candidate),
                        )
                    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — check owns parse errors
                        parsed_by_path[candidate] = None
                parsed = parsed_by_path[candidate]
                if parsed is None:
                    continue
                declarations = [parsed.module, *parsed.all_declarations]
                owners = [
                    declaration
                    for declaration in declarations
                    if declaration.location is not None
                    and declaration.location.start_line <= raw_line
                    <= declaration.location.end_line
                ]
                if not owners:
                    continue
                owner = min(
                    owners,
                    key=lambda declaration: (
                        declaration.location.end_line
                        - declaration.location.start_line
                    ),
                )
                finding["owner"] = f"{parsed.module.name}::{owner.name}"
                finding["owner_intent"] = str(owner.intent)

nerve check_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Validates .s0ma source without emitting artifacts and returns structured diagnostics with per-finding remediation — the agent's inner loop"
    expects: "path (file or directory) or project_root or both are provided"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key; never raises across the MCP boundary"
    forbids: "Never emits artifacts while it validates source, and never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: check
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=true", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Check s0ma source"
    depends_on:
        _run_cli: {relation: CALLS, carries: "s0ma check argv", coupling: TIGHT}
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def check_tool(
        path: str | None = None,
        project_root: str | None = None,
        mode: str = "strict",
        gate_mode: str | None = None,
        adoption_policy: str | None = None,
        expected_adoption_policy_hash: str | None = None,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """After editing intent or code, validate before building.

        Example: check(project_root="/work/app", mode="strict") returns
        structured findings with owner, owner_intent, and remediation; fix
        each finding, then follow next_actions. Add gate_mode="release" only
        when the caller intends to evaluate the frozen release policy.
        Read-only and closed-world.

        Args:
            path: A .s0ma file or directory. Omit to check every
                configured source_roots + test_roots entry of the
                project at project_root.
            project_root: Explicit project root for [tool.s0ma]
                discovery; defaults to the path's own directory.
            mode: Strictness mode (default strict — warnings in dev
                become errors here, matching CI).
            gate_mode: Optional caller-selected evaluation policy
                (migration, dev, strict, or release). When omitted, this
                remains a diagnostic-only check. It is never read from the
                project's authoring mode.
            adoption_policy: Optional explicit path to a
                s0ma-adoption-policy@1 document. Project-local
                adoption_exempt entries classify only and never waive debt.
            expected_adoption_policy_hash: Optional normalized sha256 pin;
                requires adoption_policy and refuses on mismatch.
        """
        if path is None and project_root is None:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "error": "check requires a path, a project_root, or both",
            }

        target = Path(path).expanduser() if path is not None else None
        if target is not None and not target.exists():
            return {"ok": False, "error": f"path does not exist: {path}"}

        root, root_error = _resolve_root(
            project_root if project_root is not None
            else str(
                cast("Path", target).parent if cast("Path", target).is_file()
                else cast("Path", target)
            )
        )
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)

        argv = ["--project-root", str(root), "check"]
        if target is not None:
            argv.append(str(target))
        argv += ["--mode", mode, "--format", "json"]
        code, stdout, stderr = _run_cli(argv)
        result = _diagnostics_result(code, stdout, stderr, mode=mode)
        _attach_finding_owners(result.get("diagnostics"), root)
        if gate_mode is None:
            return result
        if gate_mode not in {"migration", "dev", "strict", "release"}:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "error": (
                    "gate_mode must be one of: migration, dev, strict, release"
                ),
            }
        if expected_adoption_policy_hash is not None and adoption_policy is None:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "error": (
                    "expected_adoption_policy_hash requires adoption_policy"
                ),
            }

        score_argv = [
            "--project-root", str(root), "score", "--format", "json",
            "--mode", mode, "--gate-mode", gate_mode,
        ]
        if adoption_policy is not None:
            score_argv += ["--adoption-policy", adoption_policy]
        if expected_adoption_policy_hash is not None:
            score_argv += [
                "--expected-adoption-policy-hash",
                expected_adoption_policy_hash,
            ]
        score_code, score_stdout, score_stderr = _run_cli(score_argv)
        if score_code != 0 or not score_stdout.strip():
            result["ok"] = False
            result["exit_code"] = score_code
            result["error"] = _lead_with_cause(score_stderr or score_stdout)
            result["gate_mode"] = gate_mode
            return result
        try:
            score_report = json.loads(score_stdout)
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            result["ok"] = False
            result["error"] = _lead_with_cause(score_stdout)
            result["gate_mode"] = gate_mode
            return result

        result["gate_mode"] = gate_mode
        result["score"] = score_report
        result["gate"] = score_report["gate"]
        if score_report["gate"]["passes"] is not True:
            result["ok"] = False
            result["exit_code"] = 1
        if result["ok"]:
            result["next_actions"] = [{
                "tool": "verify_host",
                "arguments": {
                    "project_root": str(root),
                    "gate_mode": gate_mode,
                },
                "reason": (
                    "Run the explicit host-interpreter half of acceptance; "
                    "the static gate alone does not prove imports or installed deps."
                ),
            }]
        return result

nerve verify_host_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Explicitly executes each built production module under the host application's selected Python and reports interpreter, dependency, and isolated import evidence without pretending project code is sandboxed"
    expects: "project_root is a real built s0ma project; python, when supplied, identifies an executable host interpreter"
    guarantees: "Returns s0ma-host-verification@1 evidence and never raises across the MCP boundary; imported trusted project code may perform arbitrary side effects"
    forbids: "Never presents the project code it imports as sandboxed, and never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: verify_host
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=false", "destructiveHint=true", "idempotentHint=false", "openWorldHint=true"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Verify imports in the app's Python"
    depends_on:
        _run_cli: {relation: CALLS, carries: "s0ma verify-host argv", coupling: TIGHT}
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def verify_host_tool(
        project_root: str,
        python: str | None = None,
        gate_mode: str | None = None,
        module_timeout_seconds: float = 10.0,
        timeout_seconds: float = 60.0,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """After static checks pass, prove imports in the app's real Python.

        Example: verify_host(project_root="/work/app",
        python="/work/app/.venv/bin/python", gate_mode="release") returns a
        nested s0ma-host-verification@1 document with interpreter, dependency,
        and isolated import evidence. DANGER: this imports trusted project code
        without a sandbox; it may perform arbitrary side effects. Explicitly
        approve execution first. Not read-only, potentially destructive,
        non-idempotent, and open-world.

        Args:
            project_root: Explicit root containing pyproject.toml and build.
            python: Explicit host Python executable. Omit to resolve
                [tool.s0ma].venv_path, then platform .venv, else refuse.
            gate_mode: Optional caller-selected gate policy. Omit for a
                report-only probe whose gate.passes is null.
            module_timeout_seconds: Per-module import timeout.
            timeout_seconds: Whole-run timeout.
        """
        root, root_error = _resolve_root(project_root)
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)
        if gate_mode is not None and gate_mode not in {
            "migration", "dev", "strict", "release",
        }:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "error": "gate_mode must be one of: migration, dev, strict, release",
            }

        argv = [
            "--project-root", str(root), "verify-host", "--format", "json",
            "--module-timeout", str(module_timeout_seconds),
            "--timeout", str(timeout_seconds),
        ]
        if python is not None:
            argv += ["--python", python]
        if gate_mode is not None:
            argv += ["--gate-mode", gate_mode]
        code, stdout, stderr = _run_cli(argv)
        payload = _parse_json_payload(stdout)
        if not isinstance(payload, dict):
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "exit_code": code,
                "error": _lead_with_cause(stderr or stdout) or f"exit code {code}",
            }
        payload["exit_code"] = code
        paired_mode = gate_mode or "release"
        payload["next_actions"] = [{
            "tool": "check",
            "arguments": {
                "project_root": str(root),
                "gate_mode": paired_mode,
            },
            "reason": (
                "Run or confirm the static half under the same caller-pinned "
                "policy; host verification alone is not release readiness."
            ),
        }]
        return cast("dict[str, Any]", payload)

helper _module_neighbor_actions :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Reads the canonical build manifest and IR imports to turn one inspected module's depends-on and depended-by project neighbors into structured inspect_module next actions."
    ---
    def _module_neighbor_actions(
        root: Path,
        module: str,
    ) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
        """Return canonical module-neighbor inspection actions."""
        try:
            project = S0maProject.discover(root)
            manifest = load_manifest(Path(project.build_dir))
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — inspection fallback stays available
            return []
        module_names = {str(entry.name) for entry in manifest.modules}
        dependencies: dict[str, set[str]] = {name: set() for name in module_names}
        for entry in manifest.modules:
            try:
                document = json.loads(
                    (Path(project.build_dir) / entry.ir).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
                )
            except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
                continue
            uses = document.get("imports", {}).get("uses", {})
            raw_values = uses.get("value", []) if uses.get("state") == "value" else []
            if not isinstance(raw_values, list):
                continue
            for raw in raw_values:
                if not isinstance(raw, str):
                    continue
                target = raw.replace("::", ".").split(".", 1)[0]
                if target in module_names and target != entry.name:
                    dependencies[str(entry.name)].add(target)

        actions: list[dict[str, Any]] = []
        for target in sorted(dependencies.get(module, set())):
            actions.append({
                "tool": "inspect_module",
                "arguments": {"project_root": str(root), "module": target},
                "reason": f"Inspect dependency neighbor {target} before changing {module}.",
                "relationship": "depends_on",
            })
        for target in sorted(
            name for name, deps in dependencies.items() if module in deps
        ):
            actions.append({
                "tool": "inspect_module",
                "arguments": {"project_root": str(root), "module": target},
                "reason": f"Inspect dependent neighbor {target} before changing {module}.",
                "relationship": "depended_by",
            })
        return actions

helper _derived_adopted_surface :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Finds a named roster-adopted Python record and asks the DSL static deriver for its provenance-marked draft, never importing the target and never presenting the result as canonical IR."
    ---
    def _derived_adopted_surface(
        root: Path,
        module: str,
    ) -> dict[str, Any] | None:
        """Return the named adopted module's static draft, if rostered."""
        try:
            project = S0maProject.discover(root)
            records = load_adopted_files(Path(project.root))
        except (S0maProjectNotFound, AdoptedRecordError, ValueError):
            return None
        for record in records.values():
            source = (Path(project.root) / record.path).resolve()
            candidate_name = source.parent.name if source.name == "__init__.py" else source.stem
            if candidate_name != module:
                continue
            try:
                return derive_python_surface(Path(project.root), source)
            except DerivedSurfaceError:
                return None
        return None

nerve inspect_module_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Surfaces a built module's tagged .s0ma.json IR self-model (value/none/pending states preserved), or a per-module survey of the whole project when no module is named"
    expects: "project_root is a directory; module, when given, names a built module"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key and preserves canonical semantic-state tags; never raises across the MCP boundary"
    forbids: "Never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: inspect_module
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=true", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Inspect the self-model"
    depends_on:
        _run_cli: {relation: CALLS, carries: "s0ma inspect argv", coupling: TIGHT}
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def inspect_module_tool(
        project_root: str,
        module: str | None = None,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """Before grepping for where X is handled, inspect the self-model.

        Example: inspect_module(project_root="/work/app", module="billing")
        returns canonical tagged IR plus structured depends_on/depended_by
        neighbor calls. For roster-only Python it instead returns an exact
        DERIVED (unverified) banner and s0ma-derived-surface@1 provenance; that
        draft never satisfies a score or gate. Omit module for a survey.
        Read-only and closed-world.

        Args:
            project_root: The project to query (explicit root).
            module: Module name as declared in its .s0ma header; omit
                for the whole-project survey.
        """
        root, root_error = _resolve_root(project_root)
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)

        argv = ["--project-root", str(root), "inspect"]
        argv += [module] if module is not None else ["--all"]
        argv += ["--format", "json"]
        code, stdout, stderr = _run_cli(argv)

        payload = _parse_json_payload(stdout)
        if module is not None and code != 0:
            derived_surface = _derived_adopted_surface(root, module)
            if derived_surface is not None:
                return {
                    "ok": True,
                    "exit_code": 0,
                    "module": module,
                    "banner": _DERIVED_BANNER,
                    "derived_surface": derived_surface,
                    "next_actions": [{
                        "tool": "scaffold_module",
                        "arguments": {
                            "project_root": str(root),
                            "module_name": module,
                        },
                        "reason": (
                            "Replace this unverified roster draft with an "
                            "intent-authored canonical component."
                        ),
                        "relationship": "componentize",
                    }],
                }
        result: dict[str, Any] = {"ok": code == 0, "exit_code": code}
        if module is not None:
            result["module"] = module
            result["ir"] = payload
            result["next_actions"] = _module_neighbor_actions(root, module)
        else:
            result["modules"] = payload
        if code != 0:
            # P2: lead with the actual cause, not an advisory line.
            result["error"] = (
                _lead_with_cause(stderr or stdout) or f"exit code {code}"
            )
        return result

nerve describe_server_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Answers 'what is this server and what can it do' from the live self-model: the registered component graph plus an LLM-ready text rendering"
    expects: "the s0ma_mcp self-model registry is importable"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key; never raises across the MCP boundary"
    forbids: "Never describes a component graph that can drift from the MCP surface this server serves, and never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: describe_server
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=true", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Describe this server"
        mcp_dependency: ["self_registry"]
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    depends_on: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def describe_server_tool() -> dict[str, Any]:
        """When you do not know which s0ma surface fits, ask the server.

        Example: describe_server() returns the live tool name-to-intent map,
        situation-to-tool journeys, survival rules, and the server's own
        component graph. Choose from this response rather than guessing a tool
        or argument name. Read-only and closed-world.
        """
        return {
            "ok": True,
            # P2: lead with the rules, then the surface inventory. No
            # project_root is in scope here, so there is no presence to
            # check and no narration line: this tool answers about the
            # SERVER, not about a project (see _studi0_watching).
            "rules": _survival_rules(),
            "tool_map": live_tool_map(),
            "journeys": tool_journey_map(),
            **self_model_document(),
            "explain_for_llm": self_model_text(),
        }

helper _related_topics :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Finds the topics one topic names, by reading the ids out of its own facts and keeping the ones this build actually addresses. Derived rather than authored: a concept names the concepts it relates to, a journey names the surfaces it walks and the concepts it teaches, a document names what it covers — so 'what should I read next' is already written in the record and does not need a second list beside it that somebody has to keep in step. Authority ids are skipped because they ride the answer's own 'Decided by' line, and the id must be in the index before it is offered: a pointer to something this build cannot answer about is worse than no pointer."
    ---
    def _related_topics(index: Any, view: Any) -> list[str]:
        """The topics ``view`` names, in the order its facts name them."""
        found: list[str] = []
        for value in view.topical().values():
            parts = value if isinstance(value, tuple) else (value,)
            for part in parts:
                for word in str(part).split():
                    candidate = word.strip("`'\",.;:()[]<>")
                    if (
                        ":" not in candidate
                        or candidate.startswith("authority:")
                        or candidate == view.id
                        or candidate in found
                        or candidate not in index.views
                    ):
                        continue
                    found.append(candidate)
        return found[:6]

helper _docs_resource_for :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Names the s0ma://docs resource that carries the group a topic belongs to, so an answer about one concept can point at the resource that lists them all. Three groups have a resource of their own and everything else starts at the index — which is exactly what the index says about itself, so the two accounts cannot disagree."
    ---
    def _docs_resource_for(group: str) -> str:
        """The docs resource a reader of one ``group`` topic should hold."""
        if group == "concepts":
            return "s0ma://docs/concepts"
        if group == "journeys":
            return "s0ma://docs/journeys"
        return "s0ma://docs/index"

helper _topic_not_resolved :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Answers the two shapes a question that reached no single topic takes, and answers them differently because they are different facts: several candidates of equal standing, which the resolver returns rather than picks because picking between equals is a guess; or nothing at all, which comes back with the nearest ids this build does address so the next ask is a real one. Both carry a next action, so a miss is a step rather than a dead end."
    ---
    def _topic_not_resolved(
        index: Any,
        asked: str,
        resolution: Any,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """The answer to a question that named no one topic."""
        state = resolution.state()
        if state == "ambiguous":
            candidates = [
                {"topic_id": item.id, "category": item.group, "why": item.reason}
                for item in resolution.candidates
            ]
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "state": state,
                "asked": asked,
                "error": (
                    f"{asked!r} could mean {len(candidates)} different topics, "
                    f"and choosing between equals would be a guess."
                ),
                "candidates": candidates,
                "remediation": (
                    "Ask again with the id of the one you meant — every "
                    "candidate above carries it."
                ),
                "next_actions": [{
                    "tool": "learn_s0ma",
                    "arguments": {"topic": candidates[0]["topic_id"]},
                    "reason": (
                        f"{candidates[0]['topic_id']} is the first of the "
                        f"topics {asked!r} could have meant."
                    ),
                }],
            }
        finding = resolution.finding
        near = list(nearest(index, asked))
        answer: dict[str, Any] = {
            "ok": False,
            "state": state,
            "asked": asked,
            "code": finding.code if finding is not None else "",
            "error": finding.message if finding is not None else "",
            "remediation": finding.remediation if finding is not None else "",
            "nearest": near,
        }
        if near:
            answer["next_actions"] = [{
                "tool": "learn_s0ma",
                "arguments": {"topic": near[0]},
                "reason": (
                    f"{near[0]} is the nearest thing this build "
                    f"addresses to {asked!r}."
                ),
            }]
        return answer

nerve learn_s0ma_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Answers one question about s0ma itself from the documentation snapshot packaged with this release: resolves the asked-for topic by exact id, then by name, then by category, renders the bounded answer with its own build identity, and names both the committed page and the resource carrying the rest"
    expects: "topic is a non-empty question — a topic id, a term, or a category word"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key; never raises across the MCP boundary; offline and deterministic — the same question returns the same answer from the same build, with no model and no network in the path"
    forbids: "Never calls a model or reaches the network to answer, and never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: learn_s0ma
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=true", "openWorldHint=false", "idempotentHint=true"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Ask about s0ma"
        mcp_dependency: ["s0ma://docs/index", "s0ma://docs/concepts", "s0ma://docs/journeys"]
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    depends_on: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def learn_s0ma_tool(topic: str) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """Ask s0ma one question about s0ma, and get a short answer back.

        Example: learn_s0ma(topic="concept:ganglion") returns what a
        ganglion is, which release and documentation snapshot the answer
        came from, what decided it, the topics to read next, and the page
        carrying the full account. A bare term works too ("ganglion"), and
        a question this build cannot place comes back with the nearest ids
        it does address instead of a guess. Read-only, offline, and the
        same question always returns the same answer from the same build.
        """
        index = resources.docs_topic_index()
        identity = resources.docs_snapshot_identity()
        asked = topic.strip()
        if not asked:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "error": "learn_s0ma needs a topic to look up.",
                "remediation": (
                    "Ask for a topic id, a term, or a category: "
                    'learn_s0ma(topic="concept:ganglion"). '
                    "s0ma://docs/index lists every category this build "
                    "addresses, with an id you can copy from each."
                ),
                **identity,
            }

        resolution = resolve(index, asked)
        view = resolution.topic
        if view is None:
            return {**_topic_not_resolved(index, asked, resolution), **identity}

        rendered = render_agent(view)
        related = _related_topics(index, view)
        result: dict[str, Any] = {
            "ok": True,
            "state": resolution.state(),
            "asked": asked,
            "topic_id": view.id,
            "category": view.group,
            # The bounded answer, word for word from the snapshot. It
            # carries its own identity line, so an answer pasted out of
            # this result still says which build it came from.
            "answer": rendered,
            "answer_bytes": len(rendered.encode("utf-8")),
            "answer_budget_bytes": AGENT_BYTE_BUDGET,
            **identity,
            "authority_refs": list(view.items("authority_refs")),
            "related_topics": related,
            "read_more": {
                "page": resources.docs_page_for(view.id),
                "resource": _docs_resource_for(view.group),
            },
        }
        # An over-budget answer is a fact about the writing, not a failure
        # of the reader: the answer is still correct and still served, and
        # the finding names the field an author has to shorten.
        oversized = check_budget(view, rendered)
        if oversized:
            result["budget_findings"] = [
                {
                    "code": item.code,
                    "message": item.message,
                    "remediation": item.remediation,
                }
                for item in oversized
            ]
        if related:
            result["next_actions"] = [{
                "tool": "learn_s0ma",
                "arguments": {"topic": related[0]},
                "reason": (
                    f"{view.id} names {related[0]}; reading it next is how "
                    f"the model itself connects them."
                ),
            }]
        return result

helper _collect_artifacts :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Locates the artifacts a successful transpile emitted for the given sources — the build manifest, when present, is the authoritative record, with a stem-based scan of candidate directories covering manifest-less cases."
    ---
    def _collect_artifacts(
        root: Path,
        sources: list[Path],
        out_dir: Path | None,
    ) -> list[str]:
        """Locate the artifacts a successful transpile emitted for ``sources``.

        The CLI resolves its output directory as: explicit ``--output-dir``,
        else the resolved project's ``[tool.s0ma].build_dir`` (flat layout
        keyed by *declared module name*), else alongside each source. The
        build manifest, when present, is the authoritative record — entries
        are matched back to our input sources; a stem-based scan of the
        candidate directories covers manifest-less cases.
        """
        wanted = {s.expanduser().resolve() for s in sources}
        candidate_dirs: list[Path] = []
        if out_dir is not None:
            candidate_dirs.append(out_dir)
        else:
            try:
                project = S0maProject.discover(root)
                candidate_dirs.append(Path(project.build_dir))
            except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — no [tool.s0ma] table
                pass
        candidate_dirs.extend(source.parent for source in sources)

        found: dict[str, None] = {}  # ordered de-dup
        for directory in candidate_dirs:
            manifest_path = directory / _MANIFEST_NAME
            if manifest_path.is_file():
                try:
                    manifest = json.loads(manifest_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
                except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
                    manifest = {}
                for entry in manifest.get("modules", []):
                    # SH-M1 (INV-5): project-resolved builds record sources
                    # project-root-relative (POSIX) — resolve those against
                    # the tool's project root, not the server's CWD. DSL-M3
                    # (F-M1-1): the recipe is the one shared dsl helper
                    # ``BuildManifest.resolve_source`` (accepts the raw dict
                    # entry directly). ``resolve()`` can raise OSError on
                    # some platforms, so the comparison stays guarded.
                    try:
                        matches = (
                            BuildManifest.resolve_source(entry, root) in wanted
                        )
                    except OSError:
                        matches = False
                    if not matches:
                        continue
                    for field in ("output", "ir", "source_map"):
                        name = entry.get(field)
                        if name and (directory / str(name)).is_file():
                            found[str(directory / str(name))] = None
            for source in sources:
                stem = source.name[: -len(".s0ma")]
                for suffix in _ARTIFACT_SUFFIXES:
                    candidate = directory / f"{stem}{suffix}"
                    if candidate.is_file():
                        found[str(candidate)] = None
        return list(found)

nerve transpile_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Compiles .s0ma source (one file or directory) into .py plus .s0ma.json IR plus .s0ma.map artifacts via the DSL pipeline"
    expects: "source_path exists; source_path and any output_dir lie inside the project tree"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key; never raises across the MCP boundary"
    forbids: "Never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: transpile
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=false", "destructiveHint=false", "idempotentHint=true", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Transpile one source file"
    depends_on:
        _run_cli: {relation: CALLS, carries: "s0ma transpile argv", coupling: TIGHT}
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def transpile_tool(
        source_path: str,
        project_root: str | None = None,
        mode: str = "dev",
        output_dir: str | None = None,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """When iterating on one source, emit its Python, IR, and source map.

        Example: transpile(source_path="/work/app/src/billing.s0ma",
        project_root="/work/app") returns structured diagnostics and emitted
        artifact paths. Use build_project for cross-module resolution. Mutating,
        non-destructive, idempotent, and closed-world.

        Args:
            source_path: A .s0ma file, or a directory of .s0ma files.
            project_root: Explicit project root; defaults to the
                source's own directory. Writes outside it are refused.
            mode: Strictness mode — migration, dev, strict, or release.
            output_dir: Artifact destination (default: next to each
                source file); must be inside the project root.
        """
        src = Path(source_path).expanduser()
        if not src.exists():
            return {"ok": False, "error": f"source_path does not exist: {source_path}"}

        root, root_error = _resolve_root(
            project_root if project_root is not None
            else str(src.parent if src.is_file() else src)
        )
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)
        if not _contained(root, src):
            return _outside_tree_error("source_path", src, root)

        out_dir: Path | None = None
        if output_dir is not None:
            out_dir = Path(output_dir).expanduser()
            if not _contained(root, out_dir):
                return _outside_tree_error("output_dir", out_dir, root)

        argv = [
            "--project-root", str(root),
            "transpile", str(src),
            "--mode", mode,
            "--format", "json",
            "--quiet",
        ]
        if out_dir is not None:
            argv += ["--output-dir", str(out_dir)]
        code, stdout, stderr = _run_cli(argv)

        artifacts: list[str] = []
        if code == 0:
            # Compose the DSL's canonical discovery rather than walking
            # again here: it returns real files only (a ``.s0ma`` dir also
            # matches ``*.s0ma``) and skips hidden subtrees, so reporting
            # cannot claim artifacts for a nested checkout of the same
            # project that the transpile itself never touched.
            sources = [src] if src.is_file() else _discover_s0ma_files(str(src))
            artifacts = _collect_artifacts(root, sources, out_dir)
        return _diagnostics_result(code, stdout, stderr, artifacts=artifacts, mode=mode)

helper _find_manifest :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Locates build.s0ma.json for a project root via the resolved project's build dir, falling back to the root itself for synthetic and legacy layouts."
    ---
    def _find_manifest(root: Path) -> Path | None:
        """Locate ``build.s0ma.json`` via the resolved project's build dir."""
        build_dir: Path | None
        try:
            build_dir = Path(S0maProject.discover(root).build_dir)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — synthetic/legacy projects
            build_dir = None
        candidates = [build_dir / _MANIFEST_NAME] if build_dir is not None else []
        candidates.append(root / _MANIFEST_NAME)
        for candidate in candidates:
            if candidate.is_file():
                return candidate
        return None

nerve build_project_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Runs a full cross-module s0ma build into the project's build dir and reports the build.s0ma.json manifest summary"
    expects: "project_root is a directory"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key; never raises across the MCP boundary"
    forbids: "Never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: build_project
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=false", "destructiveHint=false", "idempotentHint=true", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Build the project"
    depends_on:
        _run_cli: {relation: CALLS, carries: "s0ma build argv", coupling: TIGHT}
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def build_project_tool(
        project_root: str,
        mode: str = "dev",
        clean: bool = False,
        force: bool = False,
        allow_new_errors: bool = False,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """After check is clean, build the complete configured project.

        Example: build_project(project_root="/work/app", mode="dev") resolves
        cross-module edges, writes deterministic artifacts and manifest, and
        returns a health_echo for the declared runtime command. It refuses NEW
        check errors and hand-edited generated files; overrides are explicit
        and separately recorded. Mutating, non-destructive, idempotent, and
        closed-world.

        Args:
            project_root: Directory whose pyproject.toml carries the
                [tool.s0ma] table (explicit; never the server's cwd).
            mode: Strictness mode — migration, dev, strict, or release.
            clean: Remove cached transpiler output before building.
            force: Overwrite generated .py files even when they were
                edited by hand (discards those edits). Default false —
                the build refuses and reports GENERATED_FILE_DRIFT.
            allow_new_errors: Build even when the edit introduces NEW
                check errors since the last baseline (the override is
                recorded in the build manifest). Default false — the
                build refuses and lists them. Distinct from force (which
                discards hand-edits to generated files).
        """
        root, root_error = _resolve_root(project_root)
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)

        argv = [
            "--project-root", str(root),
            "build", str(root),
            "--mode", mode,
            "--format", "json",
            "--quiet",
        ]
        if clean:
            argv.append("--clean")
        if force:
            argv.append("--force")
        if allow_new_errors:
            argv.append("--allow-new-errors")
        code, stdout, stderr = _run_cli(argv)
        result = _diagnostics_result(code, stdout, stderr, mode=mode)

        manifest_path = _find_manifest(root)
        if manifest_path is not None:
            try:
                manifest = json.loads(manifest_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
                manifest = None
            if isinstance(manifest, dict):
                result["manifest"] = {
                    "path": str(manifest_path),
                    "build_dir": manifest.get("build_dir"),
                    "project": manifest.get("project"),
                    "transpiler_version": manifest.get("transpiler_version"),
                    "modules": sorted(
                        entry.get("name", "?") for entry in manifest.get("modules", [])
                    ),
                    "topological_import_order": manifest.get("topological_import_order"),
                }
        if result.get("ok") and "manifest" not in result:
            # Legacy-mode honesty (pilot-01): without [tool.s0ma] the build
            # returns three green ok:true results for work the self-model
            # machinery cannot see. Say so instead of succeeding silently.
            result["warnings"] = [
                "legacy-mode build: no [tool.s0ma] table found, so no "
                "build manifest was emitted — project_status and "
                "inspect_module will NOT see this build. Call init_project "
                "to add the [tool.s0ma] table, then rebuild."
            ]
        # M4 (0.13.0, batch-01c): a green build points at the way to verify
        # it — the declared verify command, or the nudge to declare one.
        # Ship-without-running was the common thread of the batch's worst
        # outcomes (a green build, no runtime probe). The echo rides ONLY
        # the green path (a refusal result is left exactly as shipped) and
        # only when a project resolves — a legacy-mode build (no [tool.s0ma])
        # already carries its own warning above. The wording is the single
        # canonical S0maProject.health_echo the CLI build summary renders too.
        if result.get("ok"):
            try:
                echo_project = S0maProject.discover(root)
            except S0maProjectNotFound:
                echo_project = None
            if echo_project is not None:
                result["health_echo"] = echo_project.health_echo()
        return result

nerve adopt_file_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Records a pre-existing non-generated file as interim migration intake with mandatory authored intent, then points to author_module because roster adoption is not a semantic component model"
    expects: "project_root is a directory"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key; never raises across the MCP boundary"
    forbids: "Never records a file without a non-empty, one-line authored intent, and never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: adopt_file
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=false", "destructiveHint=false", "idempotentHint=false", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Record a legacy Python file"
    depends_on:
        _run_cli: {relation: CALLS, carries: "s0ma adopt file argv", coupling: TIGHT}
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def adopt_file_tool(
        project_root: str,
        path: str,
        description: str,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """When migrating old Python, record it as interim roster debt.

        Example: adopt_file(project_root="/work/app", path="src/legacy.py",
        description="Legacy billing adapter") records its intent/hash and
        returns mapped/roster counts plus a real scaffold_module action. Never
        use this for code you just wrote; new code starts from intent. The
        fourth intake reports advisory ADOPTED_CHURN without changing a gate.
        Mutating, non-destructive, non-idempotent, and closed-world.

        Args:
            project_root: Directory whose pyproject.toml carries the
                [tool.s0ma] table (explicit; never the server's cwd).
            path: File to adopt — absolute, or relative to
                project_root.
            description: Required one-line purpose statement recorded
                with the file. This is authored semantic intent; paths,
                modes, timeouts, and other invocation options keep their
                ordinary required/optional command semantics.
        """
        root, root_error = _resolve_root(project_root)
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)
        if not description.strip() or "\n" in description or "\r" in description:
            return {
                "schema": "s0ma-tool-result@1",
                "ok": False,
                "next_actions": [],
                "error": "description must be a non-empty one-line authored intent",
            }

        # Relative paths resolve against the EXPLICIT project root,
        # never the server's cwd (the standing NFR-6 doctrine).
        target = Path(path)
        if not target.is_absolute():
            target = root / path

        argv = [
            "--project-root", str(root),
            "adopt", "file", str(target),
            "--description", description,
            "--format", "json",
        ]
        code, stdout, stderr = _run_cli(argv)

        payload = _parse_json_payload(stdout)
        result: dict[str, Any]
        if isinstance(payload, dict):
            result = payload
            result["exit_code"] = code
        else:
            result = {
                "schema": "s0ma-tool-result@1",
                "ok": code == 0,
                "exit_code": code,
                "next_actions": [],
            }
            error_text = stderr.strip() or stdout.strip()
            if code != 0 and error_text:
                result["error"] = error_text
        return result

nerve migrate_ext_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Migrates a pre-consolidation project to the .s0ma extension family and [tool.s0ma] config — dry-run by default, refuse-don't-clobber"
    expects: "project_root is a directory"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key; never raises across the MCP boundary"
    forbids: "Never changes the project unless the caller turns the default dry run off, never clobbers an existing file, and never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: migrate_ext
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=false", "destructiveHint=false", "idempotentHint=true", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Migrate old file extensions"
    depends_on:
        _run_cli: {relation: CALLS, carries: "s0ma migrate-ext argv", coupling: TIGHT}
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def migrate_ext_tool(
        project_root: str,
        dry_run: bool = True,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """When legacy extension names remain, preview and apply their migration.

        Example: migrate_ext(project_root="/work/app") returns a dry-run plan;
        review it, then repeat with dry_run=false and follow the check action.
        Collisions abort atomically. Mutating, non-destructive, idempotent, and
        closed-world.

        Args:
            project_root: The project to migrate (explicit root).
            dry_run: When true (the default), plan only — change nothing.
        """
        root, root_error = _resolve_root(project_root)
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)

        argv = ["--project-root", str(root), "migrate-ext", str(root)]
        if dry_run:
            argv.append("--dry-run")
        code, stdout, stderr = _run_cli(argv)

        lines = [line for line in stdout.splitlines() if line.strip()]
        actions = [line.strip() for line in lines if line.startswith("  ")]
        summary = lines[-1] if lines else ""
        result: dict[str, Any] = {
            "ok": code == 0,
            "exit_code": code,
            "dry_run": dry_run,
            "actions": actions,
            "summary": summary,
        }
        if code != 0:
            result["error"] = summary or stderr.strip() or f"exit code {code}"
        return result

nerve scaffold_module_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Emits a minimal known-valid .s0ma module skeleton from the DSL's own template and validates it with check before returning"
    expects: "project_root is a directory; module_name is non-empty; any output_dir lies inside the project tree"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key; never raises across the MCP boundary"
    forbids: "Never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: scaffold_module
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=false", "destructiveHint=false", "idempotentHint=true", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Start a new component"
    depends_on:
        _run_cli: {relation: CALLS, carries: "s0ma new module argv", coupling: TIGHT}
        check_tool: {relation: CALLS, carries: "generated module path", coupling: TIGHT}
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def scaffold_module_tool(
        project_root: str,
        module_name: str,
        kind: str = "SERVICE",
        layer: str = "DOMAIN",
        output_dir: str | None = None,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """When starting new behavior, create a valid intent-first component.

        Example: scaffold_module(project_root="/work/app",
        module_name="billing", kind="SERVICE", layer="APPLICATION") writes a
        checked .s0ma skeleton and returns its content, survival rules, and a
        check action. Edit intent/contracts rather than generated Python. The
        project must already be initialized. Mutating, non-destructive,
        idempotent, and closed-world.

        Args:
            project_root: The project to scaffold into (explicit root;
                writes outside it are refused).
            module_name: New module name (PascalCase or snake_case).
            kind: Nerve kind — SERVICE, FUNCTION, ADAPTER, or
                ORCHESTRATOR.
            layer: DOMAIN, APPLICATION, INFRASTRUCTURE, or PRESENTATION.
            output_dir: Target directory (default: <project_root>/src),
                inside the project tree.
        """
        root, root_error = _resolve_root(project_root)
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)

        target_dir = (
            Path(output_dir).expanduser() if output_dir is not None else root / "src"
        )
        if not _contained(root, target_dir):
            return _outside_tree_error("output_dir", target_dir, root)
        target_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

        argv = [
            "--project-root", str(root),
            "new", "module", module_name,
            "--layer", layer,
            "--kind", kind,
            "--output-dir", str(target_dir),
        ]
        code, stdout, stderr = _run_cli(argv)
        combined_lines = [
            line.strip()
            for line in (stdout + "\n" + stderr).splitlines()
            if line.strip()
        ]
        cli_warnings = [
            line for line in combined_lines
            if line.lower().startswith("warning")
        ]
        if code != 0:
            # Lead with the actual cause; the CLI's stderr can open with
            # an advisory warning line that would bury it (pilot-01: the
            # duplicate-name error arrived as "warning: Operating without
            # project discovery… File already exists: …").
            cause_lines = [
                line for line in combined_lines if line not in cli_warnings
            ]
            result = {
                "ok": False,
                "exit_code": code,
                "error": "\n".join(cause_lines + cli_warnings)
                or f"exit code {code}",
            }
            if cli_warnings:
                result["warnings"] = cli_warnings
            return result

        # The CLI derives the filename with its shared _snake_case helper
        # (consecutive capitals collapse: "CLI" -> "cli", not "c_l_i");
        # use the same helper so the path lookup cannot drift.
        path = target_dir / f"{_snake_case(module_name)}.s0ma"
        check_result = check_tool(
            path=str(path), project_root=str(root), mode="strict"
        )
        result = {
            "ok": bool(check_result.get("ok")),
            "exit_code": code,
            # Forward-slashed on every OS: this is the path the tool result
            # reports and a transcript quotes, not a filesystem argument
            # (POSIX no-op; on Windows turns src\x into src/x).
            "path": path.as_posix(),
            "content": path.read_text(encoding="utf-8") if path.is_file() else None,
            "check": check_result,
            "rules": _survival_rules(root),
            "self_prompting": (
                "After a green build, wire the app's runtime card and self_inspect "
                "adapter via s0ma://guide/self-prompting."
            ),
        }
        # Forward the CLI's onboarding warnings on SUCCESS too (pilot-01:
        # the "Operating without project discovery" hint surfaced only in
        # error payloads, so the happy path dropped the one line that
        # names the bootstrap fix).
        if cli_warnings:
            result["warnings"] = cli_warnings
        try:
            S0maProject.discover(root)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — any discovery failure: not a project
            result["project"] = False
            result["hint"] = (
                "this directory is not a s0ma project yet — the module was "
                "scaffolded, but builds here run in legacy mode and "
                "project_status/inspect_module will not see them. Call "
                "init_project to add the [tool.s0ma] table first."
            )
        return result

nerve init_project_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Makes a directory a s0ma project by writing the [tool.s0ma] table into its pyproject.toml (creating a minimal pyproject when none exists) — the bootstrap step no other tool performs, so an agent is never left scaffolding modules into a directory that is not a project"
    expects: "project_root is a directory; any existing pyproject.toml is valid TOML"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key; never raises across the MCP boundary; idempotent — an already-initialized project is an ok answer with changed: false and nothing is rewritten"
    forbids: "Never rewrites a project that is already initialized, and never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: init_project
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=false", "destructiveHint=false", "idempotentHint=true", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Set up a s0ma project"
        composes: ["s0ma_dsl.cli.new_project._tool_s0ma_table", "s0ma_dsl.project.S0maProject.discover"]
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    depends_on: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def init_project_tool(
        project_root: str,
        mode: str = "dev",
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """When project_status says false, add the canonical project table.

        Example: init_project(project_root="/work/app", mode="dev") appends a
        byte-preserving [tool.s0ma] table or creates a minimal pyproject, then
        returns survival rules and a project_status action. Repeating it is an
        unchanged success. Mutating, non-destructive, idempotent, and
        closed-world.

        Args:
            project_root: Directory to initialize (explicit root; the
                table is written to <project_root>/pyproject.toml, never
                anywhere else).
            mode: Strictness mode the table declares — migration, dev,
                strict, or release (default dev).
        """
        root, root_error = _resolve_root(project_root)
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)

        valid_modes = ("migration", "dev", "strict", "release")
        if mode not in valid_modes:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "error": (
                    f"unknown mode '{mode}'; supported: "
                    f"{', '.join(valid_modes)}"
                ),
            }

        pyproject = root / "pyproject.toml"
        next_action = (
            "call scaffold_module to create the first module, then loop "
            "check -> build_project. Compact rules: "
            "s0ma://guide/survival-card. Full cold journey: "
            "s0ma://guide/getting-started. After the first green build, wire "
            "runtime self-context via s0ma://guide/self-prompting"
        )

        if pyproject.is_file():
            existing = pyproject.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
            try:
                data = tomllib.loads(existing)
            except tomllib.TOMLDecodeError as exc:
                return {
                    "ok": False,
                    "error": (
                        f"{pyproject} is not valid TOML ({exc}); fix it "
                        "before initializing"
                    ),
                }
            tool_table = data.get("tool")
            existing_s0ma = (
                tool_table.get("s0ma") if isinstance(tool_table, dict) else None
            )
            if existing_s0ma is not None and not isinstance(existing_s0ma, dict):
                # A non-table `s0ma` value (e.g. `s0ma = "off"` under
                # [tool], or an [[tool.s0ma]] array) — appending our
                # table would write INVALID TOML. Refuse without
                # touching the file (destructiveHint=false is a
                # contract, not a hint).
                return {
                    "ok": False,
                    "error": (
                        f"tool.s0ma in {pyproject} exists but is not a "
                        f"table (found "
                        f"{type(existing_s0ma).__name__}); fix it "
                        "before initializing"
                    ),
                }
            if isinstance(existing_s0ma, dict):
                # Idempotent success — the table is already there. Prove
                # it loads (a malformed table should fail loudly here,
                # not later in every build).
                try:
                    S0maProject.discover(root)
                except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 — surface loader error
                    return {
                        "ok": False,
                        "error": (
                            f"[tool.s0ma] already exists in {pyproject} "
                            f"but does not load: {exc}"
                        ),
                    }
                return {
                    "ok": True,
                    "is_project": True,
                    "changed": False,
                    "project_root": str(root),
                    "pyproject": str(pyproject),
                    "rules": _survival_rules(root),
                    "suggested_next_action": (
                        "already a s0ma project — " + next_action
                    ),
                }
            table = _tool_s0ma_table(mode, "on")
            separator = "" if existing.endswith("\n") else "\n"
            appended = existing + separator + "\n" + table
            try:
                tomllib.loads(appended)
            except tomllib.TOMLDecodeError as exc:
                # Belt and braces: never write bytes that do not parse.
                return {
                    "ok": False,
                    "error": (
                        f"appending [tool.s0ma] to {pyproject} would "
                        f"produce invalid TOML ({exc}); fix the file "
                        "before initializing"
                    ),
                }
            pyproject.write_text(appended, encoding="utf-8")
            created = False
        else:
            table = _tool_s0ma_table(mode, "on")
            # PEP 508-safe fallback name derived from the directory
            # (ASCII-gated: str.isalnum alone is Unicode-aware and would
            # let non-ASCII through into [project].name).
            name = "".join(
                ch if (ch.isascii() and ch.isalnum()) or ch in "._-" else "-"
                for ch in root.name
            ).strip("._-") or "s0ma-app"
            pyproject.write_text(
                "[project]\n"
                f'name = "{name}"\n'
                'version = "0.1.0"\n'
                'requires-python = ">=3.11"\n'
                "\n" + table,
                encoding="utf-8",
            )
            created = True

        # The table must load back — otherwise report loudly now.
        project = S0maProject.discover(root)
        return {
            "ok": True,
            "is_project": True,
            "changed": True,
            "created_pyproject": created,
            "project_root": str(project.root),
            "pyproject": str(pyproject),
            "table": table,
            "rules": _survival_rules(root),
            "suggested_next_action": next_action,
        }

helper _declared_mode :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Reads the project's declared [tool.s0ma].mode for the project_status answer, defaulting to dev when the key is absent or malformed (the dsl's project model does not yet carry a mode field — the CLI takes --mode per command)."
    ---
    def _declared_mode(project_root: Path) -> str:
        """The project's declared ``[tool.s0ma].mode``, defaulting to ``"dev"``.

        The dsl's :class:`~s0ma_dsl.project.S0maProject` model does not (yet)
        carry a mode field — the CLI takes ``--mode`` per command — but the
        ``[tool.s0ma]`` table may declare the project's default authoring
        mode (``s0ma new project`` writes ``mode = "dev"`` per FR-DSL-5).
        Read that one key from the pyproject the discovery already resolved;
        absent or malformed maps to the documented default ``"dev"``.
        """
        try:
            with (project_root / "pyproject.toml").open("rb") as fh:
                data = tomllib.load(fh)
        except (OSError, tomllib.TOMLDecodeError):
            return "dev"
        mode = data.get("tool", {}).get("s0ma", {}).get("mode")
        return mode if isinstance(mode, str) and mode else "dev"

helper _runtime_section :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Builds the project_status `runtime` section: the declared run/health commands, the env contract summary (declared / required / missing-required, read from the LIVE process environment by NAME only — never leaking a value), the runtime state_paths that must never be reverted, and whether any runbook is declared; an undeclared runbook yields a teaching hint pointing at s0ma://project/runbook that LEADS with the not-a-fault disclaimer so a mid-diagnosis agent cannot read runbook_declared: false as a service defect."
    ---
    def _runtime_section(project: Any) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """Shape the ``runtime`` section of a ``project_status`` result (O1).

        Reads the resolved project's ops-runbook fields plus the LIVE
        process environment — presence only, by variable name. A declared
        default or a live value is NEVER read into the result (names only:
        the no-secret-leak posture). ``missing_required`` lists the
        required variables absent from ``os.environ``. When no runbook key
        is declared the section still returns, carrying a ``hint`` naming
        what to declare and pointing at the ``s0ma://project/runbook``
        resource.
        """
        env_declared: list[dict[str, Any]] = []
        missing_required: list[str] = []
        for var_name in sorted(project.env):
            spec = project.env[var_name]
            env_declared.append({
                "name": var_name,
                "required": bool(spec.required),
                "fail_fast": bool(spec.fail_fast),
                "has_default": spec.default is not None,
            })
            if spec.required and var_name not in os.environ:
                missing_required.append(var_name)

        declared = bool(
            project.run_command
            or project.health_command
            or project.env
            or project.state_paths
            or project.runbook_notes
        )
        section: dict[str, Any] = {
            "runbook_declared": declared,
            "run_command": project.run_command,
            "health_command": project.health_command,
            "env": env_declared,
            "missing_required_env": missing_required,
            "state_paths": list(project.state_paths),
            "runbook_notes": list(project.runbook_notes),
            "runbook_resource": "s0ma://project/runbook",
        }
        if not declared:
            section["hint"] = (
                "no ops runbook declared — this is a documentation gap, "
                "never a service fault: an undeclared runbook cannot break "
                "or degrade a running service, so a live failure is never "
                "caused by a missing runbook. Diagnose from observed "
                "behavior, configuration, and environment; then add "
                "run_command / health_command / state_paths / "
                "[tool.s0ma.env.*] under [tool.s0ma] and read "
                "s0ma://project/runbook. state_paths mark runtime state "
                "that must never be reverted, restored from git, or "
                "regenerated."
            )
        return section

helper _current_roster_module_row :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Classifies an adoption-ledger record as a current handwritten Python module row only when the file still exists inside the project and has not become a transpiler-owned artifact."
    ---
    def _current_roster_module_row(
        root: Path,
        record: Any,
    ) -> dict[str, Any] | None:
        """Return the current plain-Python module row, or no row.

        The adoption ledger is historical and remains fully visible through
        ``adopted_files``. The module map is current surface truth: missing
        records, non-Python text, root escapes, unreadable files, and files
        that now carry both transpiler hash headers are not roster modules.
        Content drift alone deliberately remains roster debt, matching score.
        """
        relative = Path(record.path)
        if relative.suffix != ".py":
            return None
        try:
            root_resolved = root.resolve()
            candidate = (root / relative).resolve()
            if not candidate.is_relative_to(root_resolved) or not candidate.is_file():
                return None
            content = candidate.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            return None
        if (
            "# s0ma-source-hash:" in content
            and "# s0ma-output-hash:" in content
        ):
            return None
        return {
            "name": (
                relative.parent.name
                if relative.name == "__init__.py"
                else relative.stem
            ),
            "source": str(root / relative),
            "status": "roster",
            "registration": "roster_adopted",
            "mapped": False,
        }

nerve project_status_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Answers one-call orientation: is this a s0ma project, which mode and authoring posture, what is built or stale, which tagged pending semantic states block release, and the suggested next action"
    expects: "project_root is a directory"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key and semantic_debt inventory; never raises across the MCP boundary; a non-project directory is a valid ok answer, not an error"
    forbids: "Never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: project_status
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=true", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Project status"
        composes: ["s0ma_dsl.project.S0maProject.discover", "s0ma_dsl.codegen.build_manifest.load_manifest", "s0ma_dsl.codegen.build_manifest.compute_file_hash", "s0ma_dsl.codegen.build_manifest.BuildManifest.resolve_source", "s0ma_dsl.cache.extract_module_name", "s0ma_dsl.cli.main._discover_s0ma_files"]
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    depends_on: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def project_status_tool(project_root: str) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """When landing in a tree or diagnosing it, orient in one call.

        Example: project_status(project_root="/work/app") returns project and
        build posture, mapped/roster module rows and counts, pending semantic
        debt, runtime/runbook facts, survival rules, and structured next
        actions. With run/health context it also embeds the look-inward-first
        diagnostic journey. A non-project is a valid answer pointing to
        init_project. Read-only and closed-world.

        Args:
            project_root: Directory to orient from (explicit; never
                the server's cwd). Discovery walks upward from here.
        """
        root, root_error = _resolve_root(project_root)
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)

        try:
            project = S0maProject.discover(root)
        except S0maProjectNotFound:
            # Pilot-01 F3: this string is the one s0ma response guaranteed
            # to be in context at the adoption decision moment. It must
            # name the [tool.s0ma] prerequisite, the tool that creates it,
            # and the compact guide — the old "call scaffold_module"
            # advice looped forever (scaffolding never creates the table).
            return {
                "ok": True,
                "is_project": False,
                "project_root": str(root),
                # M4 (0.13.0, batch-01c): the fresh agent landing in a
                # not-yet-a-project tree is exactly the bootstrap moment
                # that most needs the rules — carry them inline here too
                # (the 0.11.0 D1 mechanism, one canonical _SURVIVAL_RULES),
                # matching init_project's every-branch precedent.
                "rules": _survival_rules(root),
                "suggested_next_action": (
                    "not a s0ma project yet — no [tool.s0ma] table in "
                    "pyproject.toml. Bootstrap in two steps: (1) call "
                    "init_project to add the [tool.s0ma] table (minimum "
                    'source_roots = ["src"]), then (2) call '
                    "scaffold_module to create the first module. After "
                    "every edit: check, then build_project. Compact "
                    "rules: read s0ma://guide/survival-card"
                ),
                "note": _TOOLCHAIN_NOTE,
            }

        # The same discovery the CLI's check/build run: every configured
        # source root and test root (G2 guarantees the roots are disjoint).
        sources: list[Path] = []
        for discovery_root in (*project.source_roots, *project.test_roots):
            sources.extend(_discover_s0ma_files(str(discovery_root)))

        build_dir = Path(project.build_dir)
        manifest: Any = None
        try:
            manifest = load_manifest(build_dir)
        except ManifestSchemaError:
            manifest = None  # missing or unreadable — either way: not built

        entries_by_source: dict[Path, Any] = {}
        if manifest is not None:
            for entry in manifest.modules:
                # SH-M1 (INV-5): project-resolved builds record ``source``
                # project-root-relative (POSIX), resolved against the
                # project root — not the server's CWD. DSL-M3 (F-M1-1):
                # the recipe is now the one shared dsl helper
                # ``BuildManifest.resolve_source`` (was hand-copied here,
                # in s0ma_dsl.cli.doctor, and in _collect_artifacts).
                entries_by_source[
                    BuildManifest.resolve_source(entry, project.root)
                ] = entry

        modules: list[dict[str, Any]] = []
        matched: set[Path] = set()
        for source in sources:
            resolved = source.resolve()
            entry = entries_by_source.get(resolved)
            if entry is None:
                modules.append({
                    "name": str(extract_module_name(source)),
                    "source": str(source),
                    "status": "unbuilt",
                    "registration": "mapped",
                    "mapped": True,
                })
                continue
            matched.add(resolved)
            current_hash = compute_file_hash(source.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            modules.append({
                "name": str(entry.name),
                "source": str(source),
                "status": "fresh" if current_hash == entry.source_hash else "stale",
                "registration": "mapped",
                "mapped": True,
            })
        for recorded in sorted(set(entries_by_source) - matched):
            entry = entries_by_source[recorded]
            modules.append({
                "name": str(entry.name),
                "source": str(entry.source),
                "status": "orphaned",
                "registration": "mapped",
                "mapped": True,
            })

        # D2 (0.11.0): list the files registered by adoption
        # (adopt_file) with their recorded content hashes so one
        # orientation call also answers "which real files does the
        # model stand behind". A corrupt record file degrades to an
        # explicit error string (verify is the surface that fails
        # loudly on it).
        adopted_files: list[dict[str, Any]] = []
        adopted_files_error: str | None = None
        try:
            adopted_records = load_adopted_files(Path(project.root))
        except AdoptedRecordError as exc:
            adopted_files_error = str(exc)
            adopted_records = {}
        for rel_key in sorted(adopted_records):
            adopted_record = adopted_records[rel_key]
            adopted_files.append({
                "path": adopted_record.path,
                "content_hash": adopted_record.content_hash,
                "description": adopted_record.description,
                "re_adopt_count": adopted_record.re_adopt_count,
            })

        # 0.14 M3A: one-call semantic-debt inventory. ``check`` remains
        # the diagnostic owner, so a parse failure is simply skipped here;
        # successfully parsed pending fields are never hidden.
        pending_fields: list[dict[str, str]] = []
        for source in sources:
            try:
                parsed = parse_s0ma(
                    source.read_text(encoding="utf-8"), filename=str(source)
                )
            except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — check owns parse diagnostics
                continue
            for declaration in (parsed.module, *parsed.all_declarations):
                owner_name = getattr(declaration, "name", parsed.module.name)
                owner = f"{parsed.module.name}::{owner_name}"
                for field, state in declaration.semantic_states:
                    if state.state == "pending" and state.reason is not None:
                        pending_fields.append({
                            "owner": owner,
                            "field": field,
                            "reason": state.reason,
                            "source": str(source),
                        })
        pending_fields.sort(key=lambda item: (
            item["source"], item["owner"], item["field"], item["reason"]
        ))

        built = manifest is not None
        not_fresh = sorted(
            str(m["name"]) for m in modules if m["status"] != "fresh"
        )
        if not built:
            build_status = "unbuilt"
        elif not_fresh:
            build_status = "stale"
        else:
            build_status = "fresh"

        roster_modules = []
        for record in adopted_records.values():
            roster_row = _current_roster_module_row(Path(project.root), record)
            if roster_row is not None:
                roster_modules.append(roster_row)
        roster_modules.sort(key=lambda item: (str(item["name"]), str(item["source"])))
        modules.extend(roster_modules)

        # STD-M4 (usability plan): an "on" project authors components as
        # .s0ma via this server, so the suggested_next_action leads with
        # the confirmation handshake — the agent is asked to confirm it
        # will author .s0ma through the server before writing code. An
        # "off" project skips the handshake and gets the plain build/check
        # guidance. The posture is read from the resolved project record
        # (s0ma_dsl.project.S0maProject.authoring), not re-parsed here.
        authoring = str(project.authoring)
        if authoring == "on":
            suggested = (
                "s0ma project; authoring is ON — confirm you will author "
                "components as .s0ma via this server before writing code"
            )
        elif not modules:
            suggested = "call scaffold_module to create the first module"
        elif not built:
            suggested = "run build_project — project not built yet"
        elif build_status == "stale":
            count = len(not_fresh)
            plural = "s" if count != 1 else ""
            suggested = f"run build_project — {count} module{plural} stale"
        else:
            suggested = "run check"

        runtime = _runtime_section(project)
        result: dict[str, Any] = {
            "ok": True,
            "is_project": True,
            "project_root": str(project.root),
            "mode": _declared_mode(Path(project.root)),
            "authoring": authoring,
            "modules": modules,
            "module_registration": {
                "mapped": len(modules) - len(roster_modules),
                "roster_adopted": len(roster_modules),
            },
            "build": {
                "built": built,
                "status": build_status,
                "stale_modules": not_fresh if built else [],
                "manifest_path": str(build_dir / MANIFEST_FILENAME) if built else None,
                "transpiler_version": (
                    str(manifest.transpiler_version) if built else None
                ),
            },
            "adopted_files": adopted_files,
            "semantic_debt": {
                "pending_count": len(pending_fields),
                "pending_fields": pending_fields,
                "completeness_credit": 0,
                "blocks_release": bool(pending_fields),
            },
            "runtime": runtime,
            # M4 (0.13.0, batch-01c): project_status is the one tool called
            # in 36/36 bench sessions while s0ma://guide landed 0/36 — so
            # the survival rules ride the loaded channel every fresh agent
            # already hits. ONE canonical source (_SURVIVAL_RULES), the
            # 0.11.0 D1 mechanism init_project/scaffold_module/
            # describe_server use verbatim; no forked wording. The
            # presence check uses the DISCOVERED root, which is the root
            # studi0 was launched on when both are looking at one project.
            "rules": _survival_rules(Path(project.root)),
            "suggested_next_action": suggested,
            "note": _TOOLCHAIN_NOTE,
        }
        if (
            runtime["run_command"] is not None
            or runtime["health_command"] is not None
        ):
            result["diagnose_journey"] = {
                "principle": (
                    "Look inward first: begin with the application's declared "
                    "self-model and observed behavior before broad code search."
                ),
                "steps": [
                    {
                        "step": "fault",
                        "instruction": (
                            "Record the observed symptom, inputs, and current runtime state."
                        ),
                    },
                    {
                        "tool": "project_status",
                        "arguments": {"project_root": str(project.root)},
                        "reason": "Load runtime and runbook context before hypothesizing.",
                    },
                    {
                        "resource": "s0ma://project/runbook",
                        "reason": "Use declared run, health, environment, and state rules.",
                    },
                    {
                        "tool": "inspect_module",
                        "arguments": {"project_root": str(project.root)},
                        "reason": "Inspect suspect intents and dependency neighbors.",
                    },
                    {
                        "step": "observed_behavior",
                        "instruction": (
                            "Test the smallest hypothesis against the live durable "
                            "instance; preserve state and report what changed."
                        ),
                    },
                ],
            }
        if adopted_files_error is not None:
            result["adopted_files_error"] = adopted_files_error
        return result

nerve run_example_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Discovers example declarations and executes each in its own timeout-guarded subprocess via core's isolated example runner"
    expects: "project_root is a directory; source_path, when given, exists"
    guarantees: "Returns a JSON-compatible dict with an ok bool key; never raises across the MCP boundary"
    forbids: "Never runs an example in the server's own process or without a timeout, and never raises across the MCP boundary."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: run_example
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=false", "destructiveHint=true", "idempotentHint=false", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Run a documented example"
        composes: ["s0ma.examples.ExampleRunner", "s0ma.registry.ComponentRegistry", "s0ma_dsl.cli.docs_commands._parse_and_validate_project"]
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    depends_on: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    covered_by: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def run_example_tool(
        project_root: str,
        example_name: str | None = None,
        source_path: str | None = None,
        timeout_seconds: int = 30,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """When documentation claims behavior, run its executable example.

        Example: run_example(project_root="/work/app",
        example_name="checkout_happy_path") executes that declaration in a
        timeout-bounded subprocess and returns stdout, stderr, duration, and
        pass/fail evidence. This executes trusted project code: not read-only,
        potentially destructive, non-idempotent, and closed-world.

        Args:
            project_root: The project containing the examples.
            example_name: Run only this example (default: run all).
            source_path: Narrow discovery to one file or directory
                (default: the whole project root).
            timeout_seconds: Per-example subprocess timeout.
        """
        root, root_error = _resolve_root(project_root)
        if root is None:
            return cast("dict[str, Any]", root_error)

        src = Path(source_path).expanduser() if source_path is not None else root
        if not src.exists():
            return {"ok": False, "error": f"source_path does not exist: {source_path}"}

        modules, filenames, diagnostics = _parse_and_validate_project(
            str(src), StrictnessMode.DEV
        )
        blocking = [d for d in diagnostics if d.severity == "error"]
        if blocking:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "exit_code": 1,
                "results": [],
                "diagnostics": {"errors": [d.to_dict() for d in blocking]},
                "error": f"{len(blocking)} validation error(s) before example run",
            }
        if not modules:
            return {"ok": False, "error": f"no .s0ma files found at: {src}"}

        # Same pre-pass the build pipeline runs so example source spans
        # match what would be registered at import time.
        compute_doc_hashes(modules, filenames=filenames)

        registry = ComponentRegistry(persist=False)
        registrations = []
        for module_name in sorted(modules):
            s0ma_module = modules[module_name]
            basename = filenames.get(module_name, f"{module_name}.s0ma")
            for declaration in s0ma_module.examples:
                built = build_example_registration(declaration, filename=basename)
                registrations.append(
                    registry.register_example(
                        name=built.name,
                        module=built.module,
                        intent=built.intent,
                        demonstrates=built.demonstrates,
                        runs_in=built.runs_in,
                        body=built.body,
                        source_span=built.source_span,
                    )
                )

        if not registrations:
            return {
                "ok": True,
                "exit_code": 0,
                "results": [],
                "summary": f"no example declarations found at: {src}",
            }

        discovered = sorted(r.name for r in registrations)
        if example_name is not None:
            registrations = [r for r in registrations if r.name == example_name]
            if not registrations:
                return {
                    "ok": False,
                    "error": (
                        f"example '{example_name}' not found; "
                        f"discovered: {', '.join(discovered)}"
                    ),
                }

        runner = ExampleRunner(registry, timeout_seconds=float(timeout_seconds))
        outcomes = [runner.run(registration) for registration in registrations]
        results = [
            {
                "name": outcome.example_name,
                "runs_in": outcome.runs_in,
                "passed": outcome.passed,
                "duration_ms": outcome.duration_ms,
                "stdout": outcome.stdout,
                "stderr": outcome.stderr,
                "returncode": outcome.returncode,
                "timed_out": outcome.timed_out,
                "error": outcome.error,
            }
            for outcome in outcomes
        ]
        failed = [r["name"] for r in results if not r["passed"]]
        result: dict[str, Any] = {
            "ok": not failed,
            "exit_code": 0 if not failed else 1,
            "results": results,
        }
        if failed:
            result["error"] = "example(s) failed: " + ", ".join(str(f) for f in failed)
        return result

helper _feedback_shaped :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Answers whether a JSON object has the field types an s0ma-feedback@1 report has, so submit_feedback can rebuild it as a typed report without the rebuild itself raising on hostile input; deliberately types no parameter as the engine's envelope, because a signature executes at import time and one such annotation would pull the whole feedback engine to module top."
    ---
    def _feedback_shaped(payload: dict[str, Any]) -> bool:
        """Whether *payload* carries the field types the schema declares."""
        if any(
            not isinstance(payload.get(key), str)
            for key in ("schema", "category", "impact", "summary")
        ):
            return False
        for key in ("expected", "actual", "details"):
            value = payload.get(key)
            if value is not None and not isinstance(value, str):
                return False
        steps = payload.get("reproduction")
        if steps is not None and (
            not isinstance(steps, list)
            or any(not isinstance(step, str) for step in steps)
        ):
            return False
        context = payload.get("context")
        if context is not None and not isinstance(context, dict):
            return False
        block = payload.get("privacy")
        if block is None:
            return True
        counts = block.get("redacted") if isinstance(block, dict) else None
        return isinstance(counts, dict) and all(
            isinstance(key, str) and isinstance(value, int)
            for key, value in counts.items()
        )

helper _feedback_approval_request :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Renders the exact-report approval copy that the bootstrap's binding layer shows the user through MCP elicitation: the whole report verbatim from the very object that will be hashed and sent, an honest statement of the metadata travelling with it, and how many values the local redactor replaced — so the user approves what is actually sent, never a summary of something hidden. Every word is written for an intelligent non-programmer and says 'feedback data' or 'feedback report', never 'payload'."
    ---
    def _feedback_approval_request(
        payload: dict[str, Any],
        contact_default: bool,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """The approval question, its labels, and the report it is about."""
        lines = [
            "Send exactly this feedback data to the kn0wthyself team?",
            "",
            f"Category: {payload['category']} — impact: {payload['impact']}",
            f"Summary: {payload['summary']}",
        ]
        for label, key in (
            ("What you expected", "expected"),
            ("What happened instead", "actual"),
            ("More detail", "details"),
        ):
            if payload.get(key):
                lines.append(f"{label}: {payload[key]}")
        steps = payload.get("reproduction") or []
        if steps:
            lines.append("Steps to reproduce:")
            lines.extend(
                f"  {number}. {step}" for number, step in enumerate(steps, 1)
            )
        lines.extend([
            "",
            "Sent with it, and nothing else: the s0ma, Python and "
            "operating-system versions, your coding client's name, and a "
            "random installation id that is not tied to you or to this "
            "machine. No source code, no file contents, none of this "
            "conversation.",
        ])
        block = payload.get("privacy") or {}
        counts = block.get("redacted") or {}
        removed = sum(int(number) for number in counts.values())
        if removed:
            detail = ", ".join(
                f"{kind}: {number}" for kind, number in sorted(counts.items())
            )
            lines.append(
                f"{removed} sensitive value was replaced with a placeholder "
                f"before you saw this ({detail})."
                if removed == 1
                else f"{removed} sensitive values were replaced with "
                f"placeholders before you saw this ({detail})."
            )
        return {
            "message": "\n".join(lines),
            "send_label": "Yes — send this feedback data now",
            "contact_label": "May kn0wthyself contact you about this report?",
            "contact_default": contact_default,
        }

nerve prepare_feedback_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Composes and locally redacts one s0ma-feedback@1 report from the user's own words and returns the exact data plus its fingerprint — the review step of the feedback journey, and deliberately the only one that is pure: it saves nothing and contacts nothing"
    expects: "summary, category and impact carry the user's own account of the problem; mcp_client is supplied by the bootstrap binding layer, never by the caller"
    guarantees: "Never writes to disk — the installation pseudonym is read, never minted, so the read-only claim is literal — never opens a socket, and returns the redacted report byte-identical to what submit_feedback will hash, show, and send"
    forbids: "Never writes to disk, mints the installation pseudonym or opens a socket."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: prepare_feedback
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=true", "openWorldHint=false"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Prepare feedback for review"
    covered_by: ["packages/s0ma-mcp/tests/test_feedback_tools.py — purity (no ~/.s0ma writes, sockets blocked), redaction report, and hash continuity into submit_feedback"]
    depends_on: none
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def prepare_feedback_tool(
        summary: str,
        category: Literal[
            "bug",
            "friction",
            "idea",
            "documentation",
            "diagnostic_quality",
            "agent_behavior",
            "performance",
            "privacy_or_trust",
            "other",
        ],
        impact: Literal["blocked", "degraded", "minor", "idea"],
        expected: str | None = None,
        actual: str | None = None,
        details: str | None = None,
        reproduction: list[str] | None = None,
        surface_kind: str | None = None,
        surface_name: str | None = None,
        diagnostic_codes: list[str] | None = None,
        mcp_client: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """Write up feedback about s0ma for the user to review — read-only.

        Example: prepare_feedback(summary="The remediation was unclear",
        category="diagnostic_quality", impact="minor") returns the exact feedback
        data and its fingerprint. Nothing is saved or sent; show it, then submit.
        """
        client: ClientInfo | None = None
        protocol: str | None = None
        if mcp_client is not None:
            name = mcp_client.get("name")
            version = mcp_client.get("version")
            if isinstance(name, str) and name:
                client = ClientInfo(
                    name=name,
                    version=version if isinstance(version, str) else None,
                )
            declared = mcp_client.get("protocol_version")
            protocol = declared if isinstance(declared, str) and declared else None
        surface: SurfaceInfo | None = None
        if isinstance(surface_kind, str) and isinstance(surface_name, str):
            surface = SurfaceInfo(kind=surface_kind, name=surface_name)
        context = build_context(
            channel="mcp",
            client=client,
            surface=surface,
            diagnostic_codes=diagnostic_codes,
            mcp_protocol_version=protocol,
            include_pseudonym=False,
        )
        pseudonym = read_installation_id()
        if pseudonym is not None:
            context["pseudonym"] = pseudonym
        composed = build_envelope(
            category=category,
            impact=impact,
            summary=summary,
            expected=expected,
            actual=actual,
            details=details,
            reproduction=reproduction,
            context=context,
            privacy=FeedbackPrivacy(),
        )
        if composed.envelope is None:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "code": "SCHEMA_INVALID",
                "error": "This feedback report cannot be sent as written.",
                "problems": [problem.to_dict() for problem in composed.problems],
                "remediation": (
                    "Fix each listed problem with the user, then call "
                    "prepare_feedback again."
                ),
            }
        report, counts = redact_envelope(composed.envelope)
        problems = validate_envelope(report)
        if problems:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "code": "SCHEMA_INVALID",
                "error": (
                    "Redacting this report locally pushed it past a size "
                    "limit, so it cannot be sent as written."
                ),
                "problems": [problem.to_dict() for problem in problems],
                "remediation": (
                    "Shorten the affected field with the user, then call "
                    "prepare_feedback again."
                ),
            }
        payload = report.to_payload()
        digest = report.payload_sha256()
        warnings: list[str] = []
        removed = sum(counts.values())
        if removed:
            warnings.append(
                (
                    "1 sensitive value was replaced with a placeholder "
                    if removed == 1
                    else f"{removed} sensitive values were replaced with "
                    "placeholders "
                )
                + "before this report was shown to anyone. Read the report "
                "back to the user as it now stands."
            )
        if read_token().token is None:
            warnings.append(
                "This machine is not set up to send feedback yet, so "
                "submit_feedback will save a draft instead of sending. The "
                "user can set it up with: s0ma feedback enroll"
            )
        return {
            "ok": True,
            "envelope": payload,
            "payload_sha256": digest,
            "redaction_report": {
                "profile": REDACTION_PROFILE,
                "redacted": dict(counts),
            },
            "warnings": warnings,
            "next_actions": [{
                "tool": "submit_feedback",
                "arguments": {"envelope": payload, "payload_sha256": digest},
                "reason": (
                    "Show the user this exact feedback report, then call "
                    "submit_feedback with it unchanged so they can approve "
                    "sending it."
                ),
            }],
        }

nerve submit_feedback_tool :: PRESENTATION
    kind: FUNCTION
    visibility: PUBLIC
    intent: "Proves the report it was handed is the one prepare_feedback produced, obtains the user's own approval through the client's approval interaction, and only then sends it — saving a local draft on every other outcome so nothing a user wrote is ever lost and nothing they did not approve is ever sent"
    expects: "envelope and payload_sha256 are the unmodified pair prepare_feedback returned; mcp_approval carries the user's decision and is supplied by the bootstrap binding layer, never by the caller"
    guarantees: "Sends only on an explicit accept, reports a receipt only when its payload_sha256 matches what was approved, and saves a draft on refusal, cancellation, absent approval, and transport failure alike"
    forbids: "Never sends a report the user has not explicitly accepted, or one it has not proved is the report prepare_feedback produced."
    evaluates_at: FUNCTION_BOUNDARY
    serves_as: submit_feedback
    metadata:
        mcp_tool_annotations: ["readOnlyHint=false", "destructiveHint=false", "idempotentHint=true", "openWorldHint=true"]
        mcp_tool_title: "Send approved feedback"
    covered_by: ["packages/s0ma-mcp/tests/test_feedback_tools.py — accept/decline/cancel, absent elicitation capability, hash mismatch, and transport failure over an in-process client session"]
    depends_on: none
    provides: none
    returns: none
    raises: none
    interface: none
    latency_ms: none
    internal: none
    expected_exceptions: none
    consumes_via_dict: none
    declares_attributes: none
    ---
    @_safe
    def submit_feedback_tool(
        envelope: dict[str, Any],
        payload_sha256: str,
        contact_ok: bool = False,
        mcp_approval: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
    ) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """Ask the user to approve this exact feedback data, then send it.

        Example: submit_feedback(envelope=..., payload_sha256=...) — pass BOTH
        through from prepare_feedback unchanged; any edit needs a fresh one. On
        approval this sends and returns a receipt, else it saves a local draft.
        """
        report = None
        if _feedback_shaped(envelope):
            block = envelope.get("privacy")
            report = FeedbackEnvelope(
                schema=str(envelope["schema"]),
                category=str(envelope["category"]),
                impact=str(envelope["impact"]),
                summary=str(envelope["summary"]),
                expected=envelope.get("expected"),
                actual=envelope.get("actual"),
                details=envelope.get("details"),
                reproduction=tuple(envelope.get("reproduction") or ()),
                context=envelope.get("context"),
                privacy=FeedbackPrivacy(
                    profile=str(block.get("profile", "")),
                    includes_source=bool(block.get("includes_source")),
                    includes_transcript=bool(block.get("includes_transcript")),
                    redacted=dict(block.get("redacted") or {}),
                ) if isinstance(block, dict) else None,
            )
        if report is None or report.to_payload() != envelope:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "code": "HASH_MISMATCH",
                "error": (
                    "This is not the feedback report prepare_feedback "
                    "produced, so nobody can be asked to approve it."
                ),
                "remediation": (
                    "Call prepare_feedback again and pass the report it "
                    "returns through unchanged."
                ),
            }
        if report.payload_sha256() != payload_sha256:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "code": "HASH_MISMATCH",
                "error": (
                    "The feedback report and its fingerprint disagree, so "
                    "nobody can be asked to approve it."
                ),
                "remediation": (
                    "Call prepare_feedback again and pass BOTH the report "
                    "and its payload_sha256 through unchanged."
                ),
                "expected_payload_sha256": report.payload_sha256(),
            }
        problems = validate_envelope(report)
        if problems:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "code": "SCHEMA_INVALID",
                "error": "This feedback report cannot be sent as written.",
                "problems": [problem.to_dict() for problem in problems],
                "remediation": (
                    "Fix each listed problem with the user, then call "
                    "prepare_feedback again."
                ),
            }
        lookup = read_token()
        if lookup.token is None:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "code": "INVALID_TOKEN",
                "error": (
                    "This machine is not set up to send feedback yet, so "
                    "nothing was sent and nobody was asked to approve it."
                ),
                "remediation": lookup.remediation or (
                    "The user can set this machine up with: "
                    "s0ma feedback enroll"
                ),
            }
        action = "unavailable"
        approved_contact = contact_ok
        if mcp_approval is not None:
            decision = mcp_approval.get("action")
            if isinstance(decision, str):
                action = decision
            answered = mcp_approval.get("contact_ok")
            if isinstance(answered, bool):
                approved_contact = answered
        if action == "preview":
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "code": "APPROVAL_REQUIRED",
                "approval_request": _feedback_approval_request(
                    envelope, contact_ok
                ),
            }
        # The installation pseudonym is the one piece of durable local
        # state prepare_feedback refuses to create — its read-only claim
        # is literal (ADR-004 §2), so a first report from an MCP-only
        # machine travels without one. Establish it here, where the tool
        # is honestly a writer, so every later report carries the same id.
        installation_id()
        if action == "accept":
            key = new_idempotency_key()
            draft = save_draft(
                report,
                channel="mcp",
                approval_method="mcp_elicitation",
                contact_ok=approved_contact,
                idempotency_key=key,
            )
            outcome = submit(
                report,
                idempotency_key=key,
                channel="mcp",
                approval_method="mcp_elicitation",
                contact_ok=approved_contact,
            )
            receipt = outcome.receipt
            if not outcome.ok or receipt is None:
                answer = outcome.to_dict()
                answer["error"] = str(
                    answer.pop("message", "")
                    or "This feedback report could not be sent."
                )
                answer["draft_id"] = draft.draft_id
                return answer
            append_receipt(
                feedback_id=receipt.feedback_id,
                category=report.category,
                summary=report.summary,
                payload_sha256=receipt.payload_sha256,
            )
            delete_draft(draft.draft_id)
            return {
                "ok": True,
                "receipt": receipt.to_dict(),
                "replayed": outcome.replayed,
                "contact_ok": approved_contact,
                "message": (
                    "Thank you — the s0ma team received this feedback as "
                    f"{receipt.feedback_id}."
                ),
            }
        draft = save_draft(
            report,
            channel="mcp",
            approval_method="mcp_elicitation",
            contact_ok=approved_contact,
        )
        if action in {"decline", "cancel"}:
            return {
                "ok": False,
                "code": (
                    "APPROVAL_DECLINED" if action == "decline"
                    else "APPROVAL_CANCELLED"
                ),
                "draft_id": draft.draft_id,
                "error": (
                    "Nothing was sent — the user did not approve sending "
                    "this feedback."
                ),
                "remediation": (
                    f"The report is saved on this machine as "
                    f"{draft.draft_id}. To send it later, run: "
                    f"s0ma feedback submit {draft.draft_id}"
                ),
            }
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "code": "APPROVAL_UNAVAILABLE",
            "draft_id": draft.draft_id,
            "error": (
                "This coding client cannot ask the user to approve sending "
                "feedback, so nothing was sent."
            ),
            "remediation": "run: s0ma feedback",
        }

test test_read_only_tools_answer_structurally :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Release-gate verification (FR-DSL-6 coverage) of the FR-MCP-2 boundary on the read-only tools: live and invalid inputs alike come back as structured ok-keyed dicts with actionable error text, never an exception — exercised on the built module pytest imports. prepare_feedback belongs here because it is genuinely read-only: an unsendable report is refused structurally without writing a byte of the user's state, which is also what makes it safe to run in the ordinary suite on every CI OS."
    tests: [check_tool, inspect_module_tool, describe_server_tool, project_status_tool, prepare_feedback_tool]
    expects: none
    depends_on: none
    mocks: none
    ---
    def test_read_only_tools_answer_structurally() -> None:
        described = describe_server_tool()
        assert described["ok"] is True
        assert described["component_count"] > 0
        no_target = check_tool()
        assert no_target["ok"] is False
        assert "check requires" in no_target["error"]
        missing = "/nonexistent/s0ma-release-gate-probe"
        for refused in (
            check_tool(project_root=missing),
            inspect_module_tool(missing),
            project_status_tool(missing),
        ):
            assert refused["ok"] is False
            assert "not a directory" in refused["error"]
        unsendable = prepare_feedback_tool(
            summary="s" * 401, category="bug", impact="minor"
        )
        assert unsendable["ok"] is False
        assert unsendable["code"] == "SCHEMA_INVALID"
        assert unsendable["problems"]
        assert "envelope" not in unsendable

test test_learn_s0ma_answers_one_topic_within_its_budget :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Release-gate verification (FR-DSL-6 coverage) that the topic query resolves an exact id, a bare term and a category word to the same kind of bounded answer, that the answer carries the build it came from and stays inside the declared 750-byte channel budget, that the page and the resource it points at are named, and that a question this build cannot place comes back with the nearest ids rather than a guess — exercised on the built module pytest imports."
    tests: [learn_s0ma_tool]
    expects: none
    depends_on: none
    mocks: none
    ---
    def test_learn_s0ma_answers_one_topic_within_its_budget() -> None:
        answered = learn_s0ma_tool("concept:ganglion")
        assert answered["ok"] is True
        assert answered["topic_id"] == "concept:ganglion"
        assert answered["category"] == "concepts"
        assert answered["answer_bytes"] <= answered["answer_budget_bytes"] == 750
        assert "budget_findings" not in answered
        # The answer says which build it is from, in its own text and in
        # the result's keys — the same two facts, so a pasted answer and a
        # parsed result cannot disagree about which s0ma this describes.
        assert answered["release_version"] in answered["answer"]
        assert answered["snapshot_hash"] in answered["answer"]
        assert answered["read_more"]["page"] == "docs/learn/ganglion.md"
        assert answered["read_more"]["resource"] == "s0ma://docs/concepts"
        assert answered["related_topics"]
        assert answered["next_actions"][0]["tool"] == "learn_s0ma"

        # A bare term is the same question asked the way a person asks it.
        by_term = learn_s0ma_tool("ganglion")
        assert by_term["topic_id"] == answered["topic_id"]
        assert by_term["answer"] == answered["answer"]

        missed = learn_s0ma_tool("frobnicator")
        assert missed["ok"] is False
        assert missed["state"] == "unresolved"
        assert missed["code"] == "DOC_TOPIC_UNRESOLVED"
        assert missed["nearest"]
        assert missed["release_version"] == answered["release_version"]

        empty = learn_s0ma_tool("   ")
        assert empty["ok"] is False
        assert "learn_s0ma needs a topic" in empty["error"]

test test_writing_tools_refuse_invalid_targets :: PRESENTATION
    intent: "Release-gate verification (FR-DSL-6 coverage) of the FR-MCP-2 boundary on the writing/executing tools: missing roots, missing sources, and a feedback report that is not the one prepare_feedback produced all produce structured refusals without raising and without touching disk — exercised on the built module pytest imports. submit_feedback refuses on that path before it reads a token, asks anyone to approve anything, or saves a draft, so this covers its boundary without a network or a state directory."
    tests: [transpile_tool, build_project_tool, adopt_file_tool, scaffold_module_tool, init_project_tool, migrate_ext_tool, run_example_tool, verify_host_tool, submit_feedback_tool]
    expects: none
    depends_on: none
    mocks: none
    ---
    def test_writing_tools_refuse_invalid_targets() -> None:
        missing = "/nonexistent/s0ma-release-gate-probe"
        gone = transpile_tool(missing + "/module.s0ma")
        assert gone["ok"] is False
        assert "does not exist" in gone["error"]
        for refused in (
            build_project_tool(missing),
            adopt_file_tool(missing, "stray_impl.py", "Stray implementation."),
            scaffold_module_tool(missing, "probe"),
            init_project_tool(missing),
            migrate_ext_tool(missing),
            run_example_tool(missing),
            verify_host_tool(missing),
        ):
            assert refused["ok"] is False
            assert "not a directory" in refused["error"]
        forged = submit_feedback_tool(
            envelope={"schema": "s0ma-feedback@1"},
            payload_sha256="sha256:" + "0" * 64,
        )
        assert forged["ok"] is False
        assert forged["code"] == "HASH_MISMATCH"
        assert "prepare_feedback" in forged["remediation"]
        assert "draft_id" not in forged

doc-ref mcp_public_surface_reference
    intent: "Generated reference of every PUBLIC nerve the s0ma authoring MCP server registers — served tools, resource providers, and prompt renderers with their contracts — rendered from the registry so it cannot drift from the served surface."
    format: markdown
    output: docs/mcp_public_surface_reference.md
    covers: [nerve where visibility == PUBLIC]
    sections: [intent, expects, guarantees, forbids]

example describe_the_running_server
    intent: "Asks the self-hosted authoring server what it is: the describe_server tool answers from the live self-model registry that the package's own built modules populate at import."
    demonstrates: [nerve::describe_server_tool]
    runs_in: standalone
    ---
    from s0ma_mcp import tools

    described = tools.describe_server_tool()
    assert described["ok"] is True
    assert described["server"]["name"] == "s0ma-authoring"
    print(f"self-model components: {described['component_count']}")

doc-guide selfhost_walkthrough
    intent: "How the s0ma authoring MCP server hosts itself: its tools, resources, and prompts are authored as s0ma modules, registered at bootstrap, served from the self-model registry — and the running server will tell you so."
    format: markdown
    output: docs/selfhost_walkthrough.md
    covers: [nerve::describe_server_tool, nerve::self_model_resource]
    asserts: [nerve::describe_server_tool.visibility == PUBLIC, nerve::self_model_resource.visibility == PUBLIC]
    examples: [example::describe_the_running_server]
    ---
    # The server that describes itself

    Every tool, resource, and prompt this MCP server exposes is authored as a declaration in a s0ma module, transpiled to Python, and registered as a component when the package imports. The FastMCP surface is then derived from that registry: a component carrying MCP metadata is served because it is registered, so the served surface and the self-model cannot drift apart.

    Two surfaces let you watch it happen. The {{nerve::describe_server_tool}} tool answers with the live component graph plus a text rendering an agent can read directly, and the {{nerve::self_model_resource}} resource serves the same graph as a JSON document.

    {{example::describe_the_running_server}}

    The assertion in the example is the whole point: the server you are talking to is the one describing itself, from the same registry its own build registered.
